INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','easy','mcq','According to standard international guidelines, hypertension is defined as a sustained blood pressure of:','["A. ≥120/80 mmHg", "B. ≥130/80 mmHg", "C. ≥140/90 mmHg", "D. ≥160/100 mmHg"]','C','Hypertension is classically defined as a sustained systolic BP ≥140 mmHg or diastolic BP ≥90 mmHg on two or more separate occasions. This definition is used by JNC 7 and the Ghana STG. The 2017 ACC/AHA guideline lowered the threshold to 130/80 mmHg (option B), but this is not universally adopted. Option A (120/80) is the upper limit of normal (elevated/prehypertension). Option D (160/100) represents Grade 2 hypertension. Clinical pearl: Always confirm hypertension with repeat readings on at least two separate visits to exclude white-coat hypertension.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','easy','mcq','Which of the following best defines a hypertensive emergency (as opposed to a hypertensive urgency)?','["A. Systolic BP >160 mmHg with headache", "B. BP >180/120 mmHg with acute end-organ damage", "C. Diastolic BP >110 mmHg without symptoms", "D. BP >150/100 mmHg in a pregnant patient"]','B','A hypertensive emergency is defined as severely elevated BP (typically >180/120 mmHg) WITH evidence of acute end-organ damage, such as hypertensive encephalopathy, acute pulmonary oedema, acute MI, or acute kidney injury. The critical distinguishing factor is end-organ damage, not just the BP number. Hypertensive urgency is similarly elevated BP WITHOUT end-organ damage. Option A describes elevated BP with a symptom but no organ damage confirmation. Option D describes elevated BP in pregnancy, which may indicate pre-eclampsia, a separate entity. Clinical pearl: Always assess for papilloedema, new neurological deficits, rising creatinine, and pulmonary oedema before labelling a presentation as emergency vs urgency.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','easy','mcq','According to Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines, what is the preferred first-line antihypertensive for a Black African patient with uncomplicated hypertension?','["A. Amlodipine 5 mg daily", "B. Lisinopril 10 mg daily", "C. Losartan 50 mg daily", "D. Atenolol 50 mg daily"]','A','Ghana STG recommends calcium channel blockers (e.g., amlodipine 5-10 mg daily) or thiazide diuretics (hydrochlorothiazide 12.5-25 mg, bendroflumethiazide 2.5 mg) as first-line agents in Black patients with uncomplicated hypertension. ACE inhibitors (lisinopril, option A) and ARBs (losartan, option B) are less effective as monotherapy in Black patients due to lower renin activity and salt-sensitive hypertension. Atenolol (a beta-blocker, option D) is not recommended as first-line for uncomplicated hypertension. Clinical pearl: Black patients have volume-dependent, low-renin hypertension - calcium channel blockers and diuretics target this mechanism most effectively. ACE inhibitors are added when there are compelling indications such as diabetes, CKD, or heart failure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','medium','mcq','A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes presents with BP 158/95 mmHg confirmed on two visits. His urinary albumin-creatinine ratio is 42 mg/mmol (elevated). Which antihypertensive is most appropriate as first-line?','["A. Amlodipine 5 mg daily", "B. Hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily", "C. Atenolol 50 mg daily", "D. Lisinopril 10 mg daily"]','D','In a patient with hypertension AND diabetic nephropathy (microalbuminuria: ACR 42 mg/mmol), an ACE inhibitor such as lisinopril is the drug of choice. ACE inhibitors reduce intraglomerular pressure independently of their systemic BP-lowering effect, slowing progression of diabetic nephropathy and reducing proteinuria. This compelling indication overrides the general preference for CCB or thiazide diuretics in Black patients. Amlodipine (option A) lowers BP but lacks proven renoprotection. Hydrochlorothiazide (option B) can worsen glycaemic control and lacks renoprotective properties. Atenolol (option D) masks hypoglycaemia symptoms and is not first-line here. Clinical pearl: In Black patients, the CCB/diuretic preference applies only to UNCOMPLICATED hypertension. Compelling indications such as diabetes plus proteinuria, CKD, heart failure, and post-MI mandate ACE inhibitor or ARB regardless of ethnicity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','medium','mcq','A 74-year-old woman with isolated systolic hypertension (BP 172/76 mmHg) has a history of gout and is taking allopurinol. Which antihypertensive should be AVOIDED?','["A. Amlodipine", "B. Hydrochlorothiazide", "C. Losartan", "D. Methyldopa"]','B','Thiazide diuretics (hydrochlorothiazide) raise serum uric acid by reducing renal urate excretion via the URAT1 transporter, and can precipitate acute gout attacks. They should be avoided in patients with active or recurrent gout. Amlodipine (option A) is safe and effective in elderly patients with isolated systolic hypertension. Losartan (option B) is unique among ARBs in being uricosuric and actually lowers uric acid - it can be beneficial in hypertensive patients with gout. Methyldopa (option D) is a second-line agent with no adverse effect on uric acid. Clinical pearl: Losartan is the only antihypertensive with a uricosuric effect - it is the preferred agent when an ARB is needed in a patient with gout or hyperuricaemia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','medium','mcq','A 48-year-old woman presents with BP 188/116 mmHg, severe headache, and blurred vision. Fundoscopy reveals bilateral flame haemorrhages and papilloedema. What is the most appropriate immediate management?','["A. Oral amlodipine 10 mg stat", "B. Sublingual nifedipine 10 mg", "C. IV labetalol 20-50 mg over 2 minutes", "D. Oral enalapril 20 mg stat"]','C','This patient has hypertensive emergency with grade IV hypertensive retinopathy (flame haemorrhages plus papilloedema). Ghana STG recommends IV labetalol 20-50 mg as a bolus over 2 minutes as the preferred agent for hypertensive emergency. The treatment goal is to reduce MAP by no more than 25% in the first hour. Sublingual nifedipine (option B) is CONTRAINDICATED as it causes an uncontrolled precipitous BP drop that can trigger cerebral or myocardial ischaemia. Oral amlodipine (option A) and enalapril (option D) act too slowly for this emergency. Clinical pearl: The rate of BP reduction matters as much as the target. Lowering BP too rapidly causes watershed ischaemia in organs accustomed to high perfusion pressures.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old man is on amlodipine 10 mg and hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg but his BP remains 162/100 mmHg. Serum potassium is 3.1 mmol/L. What is the best next step in management?','["A. Increase hydrochlorothiazide to 50 mg daily", "B. Add lisinopril 5 mg daily and recheck potassium", "C. Add atenolol 50 mg daily", "D. Add spironolactone 25 mg daily"]','D','This patient has resistant hypertension (uncontrolled on two agents at maximum tolerated doses) with hypokalaemia. Spironolactone is the preferred third-line agent for resistant hypertension and corrects hypokalaemia by blocking aldosterone-mediated potassium excretion. It is particularly effective when occult primary hyperaldosteronism is contributing to resistance. Increasing hydrochlorothiazide (option B) is not recommended beyond 25 mg as minimal additional BP benefit occurs with increased metabolic side effects including further potassium loss. Lisinopril (option C) could worsen hyperkalaemia if renal function is impaired and does not address existing hypokalaemia. Atenolol (option D) is not preferred for resistant hypertension. Clinical pearl: Resistant hypertension on three agents including a diuretic at maximally tolerated doses should prompt screening for secondary causes, especially primary hyperaldosteronism.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','hard','mcq','A 40-year-old woman presents with BP 185/112 mmHg, hypokalaemia (K+ 2.8 mmol/L), metabolic alkalosis, and a 2.5 cm right adrenal adenoma on CT. Which test best confirms the diagnosis?','["A. 24-hour urinary catecholamines", "B. Plasma aldosterone-to-renin ratio", "C. Overnight dexamethasone suppression test", "D. Renal artery duplex Doppler ultrasound"]','B','The triad of hypertension, hypokalaemia, and metabolic alkalosis with an adrenal adenoma strongly suggests primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome). The plasma aldosterone-to-renin ratio (ARR) is the recommended screening test: an elevated aldosterone with suppressed renin (high ARR >30 with aldosterone >15 ng/dL) is diagnostic. 24-hour urinary catecholamines (option B) screen for phaeochromocytoma, which presents with episodic hypertension, headache, sweating, and palpitations. The overnight dexamethasone suppression test (option C) screens for Cushing syndrome, which causes centripetal obesity, striae, and proximal myopathy. Renal artery Doppler (option D) screens for renovascular hypertension with asymmetric kidney sizes and a renal bruit. Clinical pearl: Primary hyperaldosteronism is the most common cause of secondary hypertension. Screen all patients with hypokalaemia plus hypertension or resistant hypertension using the plasma aldosterone-to-renin ratio.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','hard','mcq','A 52-year-old man has hypertension resistant to four drugs. He reports episodic severe headaches, profuse sweating, and palpitations. Plasma metanephrines are markedly elevated. Before surgical adrenalectomy, what is the correct pharmacological preparation sequence?','["A. Start phenoxybenzamine first for 1-2 weeks, then add propranolol", "B. Start atenolol first, then add phenoxybenzamine after 1 week", "C. Start IV labetalol infusion 48 hours pre-operatively only", "D. Start amlodipine and spironolactone together"]','A','Phaeochromocytoma requires alpha-blockade BEFORE beta-blockade. If a beta-blocker is started first, it blocks vasodilatory beta-2 receptors while alpha-mediated vasoconstriction remains unopposed, potentially triggering a fatal hypertensive crisis. Phenoxybenzamine (an irreversible alpha-blocker) is commenced 1-2 weeks pre-operatively to normalise BP and restore vascular volume. Propranolol (or another beta-blocker) is added ONLY after adequate alpha-blockade to control reflex tachycardia. Option A reverses this sequence dangerously. Option C (IV labetalol) has some alpha-blocking activity but is not standard pre-operative preparation. Option D (amlodipine plus spironolactone) does not adequately block catecholamine surges. Clinical pearl: The rule is always ALPHA BEFORE BETA in phaeochromocytoma. Violating this rule can be fatal during surgical manipulation of the tumour.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','hard','mcq','A 58-year-old man with known hypertension develops sudden severe tearing chest pain radiating to the back, with BP 210/120 mmHg in the right arm and 160/90 mmHg in the left arm. CT confirms Type A aortic dissection. What is the immediate pharmacological goal?','["A. Administer IV esmolol to achieve HR <60 bpm, then add IV nitroprusside to maintain SBP <120 mmHg", "B. Give IV nitroprusside to rapidly lower BP to 90/60 mmHg", "C. Give sublingual nifedipine 10 mg and arrange urgent cardiac surgery", "D. Start oral amlodipine 10 mg and oral atenolol 100 mg"]','A','In aortic dissection, the dual goals are reducing the rate of pressure rise (dP/dt) AND lowering systolic BP. IV esmolol (a short-acting titratable beta-1 blocker) is given first to reduce heart rate to less than 60 bpm and dP/dt. IV nitroprusside or nicardipine is then added to lower SBP to less than 120 mmHg. Nitroprusside alone (option A context) causes reflex tachycardia, increasing shear stress and propagating the dissection. The BP target is SBP 100-120 mmHg, not 90/60 mmHg. Sublingual nifedipine (option C) is contraindicated and causes uncontrolled BP drop. Oral agents (option D) act too slowly for this emergency. Clinical pearl: Aortic dissection management rule - BETA-BLOCKER FIRST to control heart rate and dP/dt, THEN add a vasodilator for additional BP reduction. Type A dissection requires emergency surgery.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','easy','mcq','What is the key ECG finding that defines STEMI and distinguishes it from NSTEMI?','["A. ST depression in anterior leads", "B. T-wave inversion in V1-V4", "C. New LBBB or ST elevation ≥1 mm in two contiguous limb leads (≥2 mm precordial)", "D. Prolonged QT interval >500 ms"]','C','STEMI is defined by new ST elevation ≥1 mm in two or more contiguous limb leads, or ≥2 mm in two contiguous precordial leads, or new left bundle branch block. NSTEMI presents with ST depression or T-wave inversion without ST elevation but with troponin rise. T-wave inversion in V1-V4 (option C) is typical of NSTEMI or Wellens syndrome. ST depression (option A) characterises NSTEMI or demand ischaemia. Prolonged QT (option D) is a repolarisation abnormality unrelated to STEMI definition. Clinical pearl: New LBBB in the context of chest pain should be treated as STEMI equivalent. The Sgarbossa criteria help identify underlying MI in LBBB: concordant ST elevation ≥1 mm is the most specific finding.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','easy','mcq','Cardiac troponin typically begins to rise within what time frame after onset of myocardial infarction?','["A. 30 minutes", "B. 3-6 hours", "C. 1-2 hours", "D. 12-24 hours"]','B','Cardiac troponin I and T begin to rise 3-6 hours after the onset of myocardial necrosis, peak at 12-24 hours, and remain elevated for 7-14 days. This delayed rise is why a single normal troponin at presentation does not exclude MI. Serial troponins at 3 or 6 hours are required. High-sensitivity troponin assays can detect early rises within 1-2 hours in some rapid rule-out protocols. Myoglobin rises earliest (1-2 hours) but is non-cardiac-specific. CK-MB rises at 4-6 hours and falls faster than troponin. Clinical pearl: A rising delta-troponin (greater than 20% relative rise or absolute rise above the 99th percentile URL between 0 and 3 hour samples) is more diagnostically significant than any single value.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','easy','mcq','A 55-year-old man presents with crushing central chest pain and ST elevation in leads V1-V4. Which coronary artery is most likely occluded?','["A. Right coronary artery", "B. Left anterior descending artery", "C. Left circumflex artery", "D. Posterior descending artery"]','B','ST elevation in V1-V4 (anterior leads) indicates anterior STEMI, caused by occlusion of the left anterior descending (LAD) artery. The LAD supplies the anterior wall, interventricular septum, and cardiac apex. Right coronary artery (RCA) occlusion causes inferior STEMI with ST elevation in II, III, and aVF. Left circumflex (LCx) occlusion causes lateral STEMI with changes in I, aVL, V5-V6. Posterior descending artery involvement causes posterior MI with reciprocal ST depression and tall R waves in V1-V3. Clinical pearl: Anterior STEMI (LAD) carries the worst prognosis due to the large myocardial territory at risk. It is the most common cause of cardiogenic shock and ventricular aneurysm post-MI.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','medium','mcq','A 64-year-old woman with 2 hours of central chest pain and diaphoresis has 2 mm ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. According to Ghana STG, what is the correct immediate pharmacological management bundle?','["A. Aspirin 75 mg + atorvastatin + oxygen + IV morphine only", "B. Aspirin 300 mg + warfarin + IV heparin + sublingual GTN", "C. Clopidogrel 75 mg daily + enoxaparin + IV atenolol + oxygen", "D. Aspirin 300 mg + clopidogrel 300 mg + sublingual GTN + IV morphine + enoxaparin SC"]','D','Inferior STEMI management per Ghana STG requires the MONA-CE bundle: Morphine IV (2.5-5 mg for analgesia), Oxygen (if SpO2 <94%), Nitrates (sublingual GTN 0.4 mg, withhold if SBP <90 mmHg), Aspirin 300 mg loading dose, Clopidogrel 300 mg loading dose, and Enoxaparin SC 1 mg/kg. Option A gives an inadequate aspirin dose and omits clopidogrel and anticoagulation. Option C incorrectly adds warfarin, which is not part of acute STEMI protocol. Option D uses a subtherapeutic clopidogrel maintenance dose of 75 mg instead of the 300 mg loading dose. Clinical pearl: Loading doses of dual antiplatelet therapy are essential in STEMI. Aspirin 300 mg and clopidogrel 300 mg inhibit platelet aggregation synergistically via different mechanisms (COX-1 and P2Y12 pathways respectively).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','medium','mcq','A 60-year-old man is admitted with NSTEMI. Troponin is rising. He is on aspirin, clopidogrel, and enoxaparin. Which additional long-term therapy specifically reduces post-MI ventricular remodelling?','["A. Digoxin 0.125 mg daily", "B. Amiodarone 200 mg daily", "C. Ramipril 5 mg daily", "D. Furosemide 40 mg daily"]','C','ACE inhibitors such as ramipril (or lisinopril) reduce post-MI ventricular remodelling by blocking angiotensin II-mediated myocardial hypertrophy and fibrosis. They reduce mortality post-MI, particularly in patients with anterior MI, reduced ejection fraction, or heart failure. Per Ghana STG, they should be commenced within 24 hours of MI if SBP is greater than 100 mmHg. Digoxin (option A) is used for rate control in AF or systolic HF and has no anti-remodelling benefit. Amiodarone (option C) is an antiarrhythmic without proven post-MI mortality benefit when used prophylactically. Furosemide (option D) treats pulmonary oedema but does not address ventricular remodelling. Clinical pearl: Long-term post-MI pharmacotherapy follows the BASH mnemonic: Beta-blocker, ACE inhibitor, Statin, aspirin plus clopidogrel (dual antiplatelet for 12 months).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old man with anterior STEMI arrives at a PCI-capable hospital. What is the maximum recommended door-to-balloon time from first medical contact?','["A. 30 minutes", "B. 60 minutes", "C. 90 minutes", "D. 180 minutes"]','C','The recommended door-to-balloon (D2B) time for primary PCI is ≤90 minutes from first medical contact when the patient presents directly to a PCI-capable centre. If primary PCI cannot be performed within 90-120 minutes of first medical contact, thrombolytic therapy should be administered within 30 minutes of hospital arrival (door-to-needle time). The 30-minute target (option A) applies to thrombolysis door-to-needle time. Option B (60 minutes) is too short for most realistic PCI workflows. Option D (180 minutes) exceeds all acceptable delays. Clinical pearl: Every 30-minute delay in reperfusion therapy increases 1-year mortality by approximately 7.5%. Rapid triage and activation of the catheterisation lab team are essential.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','hard','mcq','A 72-year-old man develops cardiogenic shock 8 hours after anterior STEMI. BP is 76/48 mmHg, HR 122 bpm, cold peripheries, and bilateral basal crackles. Which is the most appropriate initial management?','["A. Noradrenaline IV infusion to maintain MAP >65 mmHg as first-line agent", "B. IV furosemide 80 mg and oral carvedilol 3.125 mg for decompensated heart failure", "C. IV amiodarone and electrical cardioversion", "D. Dobutamine IV infusion plus urgent reperfusion strategy"]','D','Cardiogenic shock post-MI requires both haemodynamic support and urgent reperfusion. Dobutamine (a positive inotrope with mild vasodilatory effects) is the preferred agent per Ghana STG for cardiogenic shock, increasing cardiac output while reducing afterload. Urgent revascularisation (primary PCI or thrombolysis if PCI unavailable) is mandatory as it is the only intervention that improves long-term outcomes. Noradrenaline (option A) increases SVR and afterload, worsening cardiac function in pure cardiogenic shock. It is used only if profound hypotension requires vasopressor support alongside inotropes. Furosemide and carvedilol (option B) are inappropriate during acute shock. Amiodarone (option D) addresses arrhythmias but not the haemodynamic crisis. Clinical pearl: In cardiogenic shock, inotropic support with dobutamine is a bridge to definitive treatment. Emergency revascularisation is the only intervention proven to reduce mortality in this setting.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','hard','mcq','Five days after an anterior STEMI, a patient develops sudden breathlessness and a new loud pansystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border. Oxygen saturation step-up is confirmed from right atrium to pulmonary artery on catheterisation. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Acute mitral regurgitation from papillary muscle rupture", "B. Post-infarction ventricular septal defect", "C. Left ventricular free wall rupture with haemopericardium", "D. Acute aortic regurgitation from valve ring dilatation"]','B','Post-infarction ventricular septal defect (VSD) occurs 3-7 days after MI and presents with sudden haemodynamic deterioration and a harsh pansystolic murmur at the LOWER LEFT sternal border. The pathognomonic finding is an oxygen saturation step-up from the right atrium to the pulmonary artery on right heart catheterisation, confirming a left-to-right intracardiac shunt. Acute mitral regurgitation (option A) causes a pansystolic murmur heard best at the APEX radiating to the axilla, due to posterior papillary muscle rupture in inferior MI. Free wall rupture (option C) causes sudden cardiac death or tamponade without a murmur. Aortic regurgitation (option D) is an early diastolic murmur and unrelated to acute MI. Clinical pearl: Location distinguishes post-MI VSD from acute MR. Sternal border equals VSD (septal rupture), and apex with axillary radiation equals MR (mitral). Both require urgent surgical referral.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old man presents with STEMI. Which of the following is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to thrombolytic therapy?','["A. Prior haemorrhagic stroke at any time", "B. Age over 75 years", "C. Uncontrolled hypertension with SBP 185 mmHg", "D. Previous coronary artery bypass graft surgery"]','A','Prior haemorrhagic stroke at any time is an absolute contraindication to thrombolysis due to the unacceptable risk of catastrophic cerebral re-bleeding. Other absolute contraindications include: active internal bleeding excluding menstruation, suspected aortic dissection, significant closed-head trauma within 3 months, previous ischaemic stroke within 3 months, and intracranial neoplasm. Age >75 years (option A) is a relative contraindication. Uncontrolled hypertension >180/110 mmHg (option C) is a relative contraindication. Previous CABG (option D) is not a contraindication. Clinical pearl: When absolute contraindications to thrombolysis exist, primary PCI becomes the mandatory reperfusion strategy. Transfer the patient to a PCI-capable centre without delay.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','hard','mcq','An inferior STEMI patient has ST elevation in II, III, aVF with ST depression and tall dominant R waves in V1-V3. ST elevation is also seen in lead V4R. What do these findings together indicate?','["A. Posterior MI and right ventricular infarction", "B. Anterior STEMI with lateral extension", "C. Left bundle branch block pattern", "D. Wellens syndrome pattern"]','A','ST depression with tall R waves (R>S) in V1-V3 represents the reciprocal mirror image of posterior ST elevation, confirming posterior MI extension of the inferior STEMI. Posterior leads V7-V9 would demonstrate ST elevation. ST elevation in V4R (right-sided lead) confirms right ventricular (RV) infarction, which occurs in approximately 40% of inferior STEMIs due to proximal RCA occlusion. This combination has critical management implications: RV infarction requires volume loading as RV filling pressure depends on preload. Avoid nitrates and diuretics in RV MI as they reduce preload and can cause precipitous hypotension. Anterior STEMI (option A) has changes in V1-V4 with small R waves. LBBB (option B) shows QRS morphology changes, not isolated ST changes. Wellens syndrome (option D) shows characteristic T-wave changes in V2-V3 in a pain-free patient. Clinical pearl: In inferior STEMI always check V4R and V7-V9 to identify RV and posterior extension, as management differs significantly.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','easy','mcq','A patient gets central chest tightness when climbing more than one flight of stairs at a normal pace but has no symptoms at rest or on gentle walking. What is the Canadian Cardiovascular Society (CCS) angina class?','["A. CCS Class I", "B. CCS Class III", "C. CCS Class IV", "D. CCS Class II"]','D','CCS Class II angina is defined as slight limitation of ordinary physical activity: angina occurs when walking more than two blocks on level ground, climbing more than one flight of stairs at a normal pace, or walking uphill. CCS Class I produces no limitation with ordinary activity - angina only with unusually strenuous exertion. CCS Class III is marked limitation - angina on walking one to two blocks on level ground or climbing one flight of stairs. CCS Class IV is inability to perform any activity without discomfort, or angina at rest. Clinical pearl: The CCS classification guides treatment escalation. Class III-IV symptoms despite optimal medical therapy are an indication to consider revascularisation with PCI or CABG.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','easy','mcq','Which of the following best describes typical stable angina?','["A. Sharp stabbing left-sided chest pain reproduced by palpation", "B. Central chest pressure precipitated by exertion, relieved within 5 minutes by rest or sublingual GTN", "C. Persistent chest pain lasting more than 20 minutes unrelieved by GTN", "D. Left-sided chest pain worsening on deep inspiration and lying flat"]','B','Typical stable angina is characterised by three features: substernal chest discomfort with a characteristic quality such as pressure, squeezing, or heaviness; provoked by exertion or emotional stress; and relieved by rest or sublingual nitrates within 5 minutes. All three features must be present for typical angina. Option A describes musculoskeletal pain that is reproducible on palpation. Option C describes unstable angina or NSTEMI, with prolonged rest pain unrelieved by GTN. Option D describes pleuritic pain suggesting pericarditis or pulmonary embolism. Clinical pearl: Angina with only two of three features is called atypical angina. One or no features is non-cardiac chest pain. Women and diabetics more often present atypically with dyspnoea or fatigue.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','easy','mcq','Which of the following is the strongest independent non-modifiable risk factor for coronary artery disease?','["A. Obesity", "B. Family history of premature CAD in a first-degree relative", "C. Physical inactivity", "D. Hypercholesterolaemia"]','B','Family history of premature CAD (first-degree male relative under 55 years or female relative under 65 years) is among the strongest non-modifiable independent risk factors for CAD and is used in cardiovascular risk scores. Cigarette smoking, diabetes, hypertension, and hypercholesterolaemia are major modifiable risk factors. Obesity (option A) and physical inactivity (option B) are significant modifiable risk factors operating partly through their effects on BP, lipids, and glucose tolerance. Hypercholesterolaemia (option C) is a major modifiable risk factor but not the strongest non-modifiable one. Clinical pearl: A positive family history increases calculated cardiovascular risk by approximately 1.5-2 fold above standard model estimates. It cannot be modified but intensifies the need to control all other risk factors.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','easy','mcq','Which of the following is a risk equivalent to established coronary artery disease when calculating cardiovascular risk?','["A. Metabolic syndrome", "B. Hypertension with left ventricular hypertrophy", "C. Age greater than 65 years", "D. Diabetes mellitus with target organ damage"]','D','Diabetes mellitus with target organ damage (diabetic nephropathy, retinopathy, or neuropathy) is considered a cardiovascular risk equivalent. Patients with diabetes and target organ damage have a similar 10-year cardiovascular event rate as patients with established CAD. Other risk equivalents include prior MI or stroke, peripheral arterial disease, abdominal aortic aneurysm, and chronic kidney disease with eGFR <30 mL/min. Metabolic syndrome (option A) increases risk but is not a direct risk equivalent. Hypertension with LVH (option B) is a strong risk factor but not a risk equivalent. Age >65 (option D) is a risk factor incorporated into risk scores but is not itself a risk equivalent. Clinical pearl: Patients with diabetes plus established CAD require the most intensive LDL lowering, with targets of LDL <1.4 mmol/L using high-intensity statin therapy such as atorvastatin 40-80 mg.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','medium','mcq','A 58-year-old man with stable angina (CCS Class II) is started on sublingual GTN for acute relief. Which drug should be added as first-line anti-anginal therapy for prevention of attacks?','["A. Verapamil 240 mg daily", "B. Isosorbide mononitrate 20 mg twice daily", "C. Atenolol 50 mg daily", "D. Nifedipine retard 20 mg twice daily"]','C','Beta-blockers (atenolol, bisoprolol, metoprolol) are first-line anti-anginal agents for stable angina. They reduce myocardial oxygen demand by decreasing heart rate, contractility, and systolic BP. They have additional evidence for reducing mortality post-MI and in heart failure. GTN sublingual is used for acute symptom relief, not prevention. Verapamil (option A) is a non-dihydropyridine CCB used as an alternative if beta-blockers are contraindicated such as in asthma or COPD, or in vasospastic angina. It should not be combined with beta-blockers due to additive bradycardia and heart block risk. Long-acting nitrates (option B) are added as second-line agents. Nifedipine retard (option D) is a dihydropyridine CCB less suitable as sole first-line anti-anginal. Clinical pearl: Per Ghana STG, stable angina is managed with aspirin, a beta-blocker or verapamil if beta-blocker is contraindicated, and sublingual GTN for acute attacks.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','medium','mcq','A 52-year-old man with stable angina has an exercise stress test showing 2 mm horizontal ST depression in V4-V6 at 5 METs with reproduction of chest pain and a blood pressure drop of 15 mmHg. What does this indicate?','["A. The test is negative as ST depression is expected at this workload", "B. The test is inconclusive and requires nuclear perfusion imaging", "C. This is a high-risk positive test suggesting significant multi-vessel disease", "D. The finding is a false positive due to left ventricular hypertrophy"]','C','This exercise stress test has multiple high-risk features: ≥2 mm ST depression (more than 1 mm is positive), occurring at low workload (5 METs), ST depression in multiple lateral leads V4-V6, reproduction of anginal chest pain, and a drop in systolic BP during exercise when normally BP should rise indicating LV dysfunction. Together these features suggest severe multi-vessel CAD and are indications for early coronary angiography with a view to revascularisation. A negative test would show no ST changes or symptoms to maximum heart rate. Nuclear imaging (option B) is used for inconclusive standard ETTs. LVH (option D) can cause false-positive ST depression but not the combination of BP drop, symptoms, and severe ST changes. Clinical pearl: High-risk stress test features include early positive at less than 5 METs, ≥2 mm ST depression, BP drop, failure to achieve target heart rate, and prolonged ST recovery. These indicate multi-vessel CAD warranting angiography.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','medium','mcq','A 60-year-old woman with stable angina on atenolol 100 mg daily continues to have weekly anginal episodes. What is the most appropriate next step?','["A. Replace atenolol with digoxin 0.125 mg daily", "B. Add sublingual GTN only when attacks occur without any other change", "C. Add amlodipine 5 mg daily to atenolol alone", "D. Add long-acting nitrate (isosorbide mononitrate 20 mg BD) and consider coronary angiography"]','D','When angina persists despite adequate beta-blocker therapy at maximum tolerated dose, the next step is to add a second anti-anginal agent such as a long-acting nitrate (isosorbide mononitrate) or a calcium channel blocker (amlodipine). In addition, persistent symptoms on medical therapy warrant coronary angiography to assess coronary anatomy for possible revascularisation. Adding amlodipine (option C) is a valid add-on but in isolation does not address the need to evaluate for revascularisation. Digoxin (option A) has no anti-anginal role. PRN sublingual GTN alone (option B) is inadequate when attacks occur weekly on medical therapy. Clinical pearl: The anti-anginal ladder is: sublingual GTN for acute attacks, then beta-blocker or CCB if beta-blocker contraindicated, then add long-acting nitrate or CCB, then coronary angiography and revascularisation consideration.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','hard','mcq','A 68-year-old man with stable angina has coronary angiography showing 90% stenosis of the left main coronary artery and ejection fraction of 45%. Which revascularisation strategy is preferred?','["A. Optimal medical therapy alone as PCI and CABG have similar outcomes in left main disease", "B. Percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) with drug-eluting stents", "C. Coronary artery bypass graft surgery (CABG)", "D. Urgent thrombolytic therapy"]','C','Left main coronary artery disease with stenosis ≥50% and reduced ejection fraction (45%) is a Class I indication for CABG over PCI. CABG provides superior long-term outcomes including survival benefit in left main disease, multi-vessel CAD with reduced EF, and in diabetics with multi-vessel disease. PCI (option B) is preferred for single or two-vessel disease without left main involvement, or high surgical risk patients. Medical therapy alone (option A) is inadequate for significant left main disease, which carries a high risk of sudden cardiac death without revascularisation. Thrombolysis (option D) has no role in stable CAD. Clinical pearl: The SYNTAX score guides PCI versus CABG decision in complex CAD. High SYNTAX scores above 32 favour CABG, especially in left main or three-vessel disease with impaired LV function.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old man with refractory angina has failed two anti-anginal drugs and is not suitable for revascularisation due to diffuse distal vessel disease. Which additional therapy may help manage his symptoms?','["A. Ranolazine (a late sodium channel inhibitor)", "B. Oral warfarin anticoagulation", "C. High-dose isosorbide mononitrate 120 mg daily as continuous therapy", "D. Spironolactone 50 mg daily"]','A','Ranolazine is a late sodium channel inhibitor approved for refractory stable angina when symptoms persist despite optimal conventional therapy with beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, and long-acting nitrates, and when revascularisation is not feasible. It reduces intracellular calcium overload during ischaemia and has a favourable safety profile with no effect on heart rate or blood pressure. Oral warfarin (option A) reduces thrombotic events but is not an anti-anginal agent. High-dose continuous long-acting nitrates (option B) cause nitrate tolerance and headaches, requiring a daily nitrate-free interval of 8-12 hours. Spironolactone (option D) is used in heart failure and hyperaldosteronism, not refractory angina. Clinical pearl: Other options for refractory angina include enhanced external counterpulsation (EECP) and spinal cord stimulation, representing a spectrum of invasive and non-invasive approaches when conventional therapy fails.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','hard','mcq','A 50-year-old woman with no cardiac history develops angina during exertion and also at rest in the early morning hours. ECG during an episode shows transient ST elevation that resolves completely. Coronary angiography is normal. What is the diagnosis and management?','["A. Prinzmetal (vasospastic) angina - start calcium channel blocker and avoid beta-blockers", "B. NSTEMI - commence dual antiplatelet therapy and enoxaparin", "C. Stable exertional angina - start atenolol and long-acting nitrate", "D. Hypertensive heart disease - optimise blood pressure control"]','A','Prinzmetal (variant) angina is caused by coronary artery vasospasm, typically occurring at rest and in the early morning hours, with transient ST elevation on ECG that resolves completely. Coronary angiography is normal or shows non-obstructive disease. The treatment is calcium channel blockers such as diltiazem, verapamil, or nifedipine retard, which are potent coronary vasodilators. Beta-blockers (option B context) are CONTRAINDICATED in vasospastic angina as they block vasodilatory beta-2 receptors, allowing unopposed alpha-mediated vasospasm to worsen. NSTEMI (option A) would show troponin elevation. Hypertensive heart disease (option D) does not explain transient ST elevation with normal coronary arteries. Clinical pearl: In vasospastic angina avoid beta-blockers, avoid smoking which is a potent vasospasm trigger, and use calcium channel blockers as first-line. Sublingual nitrates relieve acute spasm.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','easy','mcq','Which of the following is a MAJOR Framingham criterion for the diagnosis of heart failure?','["A. Ankle oedema", "B. Nocturnal cough", "C. Pleural effusion on chest X-ray", "D. Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea or orthopnoea"]','D','Major Framingham criteria for heart failure include: paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea (PND) or orthopnoea, neck vein distension, rales (crackles), cardiomegaly on CXR, acute pulmonary oedema, S3 gallop, increased venous pressure greater than 16 cmH2O, and weight loss greater than 4.5 kg in 5 days in response to treatment. Ankle oedema (option A) and nocturnal cough (option C) are MINOR criteria. Pleural effusion (option D) is also a minor criterion. The Framingham criteria require 2 major OR 1 major plus 2 minor criteria for clinical diagnosis of heart failure. Clinical pearl: PND is highly specific for left heart failure. The patient wakes 1-2 hours after sleep, sits upright or runs to an open window for relief, and it takes 15-20 minutes to settle. It occurs as gravitational fluid redistribution from lower limbs to lungs occurs after lying flat.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','easy','mcq','A patient with heart failure has symptoms on mild exertion such as walking on level ground but is comfortable at rest. What is the NYHA functional class?','["A. NYHA Class I", "B. NYHA Class III", "C. NYHA Class II", "D. NYHA Class IV"]','B','NYHA Class III is marked limitation of ordinary physical activity: comfortable at rest but symptoms of dyspnoea, fatigue, or palpitations occur with less-than-ordinary activity such as walking on level ground. NYHA Class I has no limitation - ordinary activity does not cause symptoms. NYHA Class II is slight limitation - comfortable at rest but symptoms with ordinary activity such as climbing stairs. NYHA Class IV has symptoms at rest or with minimal exertion. Clinical pearl: NYHA classification guides therapy escalation in heart failure. NYHA III-IV despite optimal therapy with ACE inhibitor, beta-blocker, and diuretic prompts consideration of spironolactone, cardiac resynchronisation therapy, or advanced therapies such as transplantation or LVAD.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','easy','mcq','Brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) is most useful in heart failure for which clinical purpose?','["A. Ruling out heart failure as a cause of acute dyspnoea due to its high negative predictive value", "B. Identifying the specific aetiology of heart failure", "C. Differentiating between systolic and diastolic dysfunction only", "D. Predicting the location of ventricular dysfunction"]','A','BNP and NT-proBNP have a high negative predictive value for heart failure. A normal BNP below 100 pg/mL effectively rules out heart failure as a cause of acute dyspnoea, making it most useful for EXCLUSION. An elevated BNP supports the diagnosis but is not specific, as it can be elevated in AF, pulmonary hypertension, PE, and renal failure. BNP cannot identify the aetiology (option A) or location (option D) of ventricular dysfunction. Serial BNP-guided therapy (option B) monitoring applies to multiple drug classes, not specifically to differentiating dysfunction types. Clinical pearl: In acute dyspnoea, BNP below 100 pg/mL rules out HF. BNP above 400 pg/mL strongly suggests HF. Values 100-400 require clinical correlation. NT-proBNP thresholds are approximately 5-10 times higher than BNP.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old woman presents with acute pulmonary oedema: severe breathlessness, SpO2 88%, bilateral crackles, frothy pink sputum, BP 160/100 mmHg, HR 110 bpm. What is the most appropriate immediate management?','["A. IV furosemide 40 mg + oral lisinopril 10 mg + oxygen alone", "B. Oral furosemide 80 mg + IV labetalol + high-flow oxygen", "C. High-flow oxygen + IV digoxin 0.5 mg + oral carvedilol 6.25 mg", "D. IV furosemide 40-80 mg + high-flow oxygen + sit upright + IV morphine 2.5-5 mg + sublingual or IV GTN if SBP >100"]','D','Acute pulmonary oedema management per Ghana STG requires: sitting the patient upright to reduce preload, high-flow oxygen to correct hypoxia, IV furosemide 40-80 mg (diuresis and immediate venodilation), IV morphine 2.5-5 mg (reduces anxiety, respiratory drive, and preload via venodilatation), and sublingual or IV GTN if SBP >100 mmHg for preload reduction. This combination rapidly reduces left heart filling pressure and relieves pulmonary congestion. Oral lisinopril (option B) acts too slowly for acute pulmonary oedema. IV labetalol (option C) could worsen cardiac function acutely. IV digoxin (option D) is not first-line for acute pulmonary oedema, and oral carvedilol is contraindicated in decompensated HF. Clinical pearl: The LMNOP mnemonic covers acute pulmonary oedema management: Lasix (furosemide), Morphine, Nitrates, Oxygen, and Position (upright).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old man with HFrEF (EF 32%) is on furosemide 40 mg daily and lisinopril 10 mg daily with NYHA Class II symptoms. What should be added next according to evidence-based guidelines?','["A. Digoxin 0.125 mg daily", "B. Spironolactone 25 mg daily", "C. Bisoprolol 1.25 mg daily (to be uptitrated slowly)", "D. Amlodipine 5 mg daily"]','C','Beta-blockers (bisoprolol, carvedilol, metoprolol succinate) are evidence-based in HFrEF and reduce mortality by approximately 34% by counteracting the harmful effects of chronic sympathetic activation. Per Ghana STG, bisoprolol or carvedilol should be added in all stable HFrEF patients already on ACE inhibitor and diuretic. They must be started at low doses and uptitrated slowly as they can worsen acute decompensation. Digoxin (option A) is added later for AF with fast ventricular rate or persistent symptoms despite optimal triple therapy. Spironolactone (option B) is added as a fourth agent for NYHA III-IV despite ACE-I plus beta-blocker, or to provide additional neurohormonal blockade. Amlodipine (option D) is not used in HFrEF unless required for hypertension or angina. Clinical pearl: Beta-blockers improve EF in HFrEF through reverse remodelling with continued use. Never withhold them due to a low EF; the EF often improves significantly with treatment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','medium','mcq','A 72-year-old man with HFrEF (EF 28%) remains NYHA Class III despite optimal doses of lisinopril, bisoprolol, and furosemide. What is the next evidence-based step?','["A. Add verapamil 80 mg three times daily", "B. Start digoxin and switch lisinopril to sacubitril/valsartan", "C. Add spironolactone 25-50 mg daily", "D. Increase furosemide to 160 mg daily"]','C','Spironolactone 25-50 mg daily is the evidence-based next step for NYHA Class III-IV HFrEF symptoms persisting despite ACE inhibitor, beta-blocker, and diuretic therapy. This was demonstrated in the RALES trial. Aldosterone antagonism provides additional neurohormonal blockade, reduces hospitalisation, and improves survival. Use with caution if eGFR is below 30 or potassium is above 5.0 mmol/L due to risk of hyperkalaemia. Verapamil (option A) is contraindicated in HFrEF as non-dihydropyridine CCBs have negative inotropic effects and worsen cardiac function. Switching to sacubitril/valsartan (option B) is an option but is not available in most low-resource settings. Doubling furosemide (option C) treats volume overload but does not improve prognosis. Clinical pearl: Spironolactone 25-50 mg daily reduces mortality by 30% in severe HFrEF per the RALES trial. Monitor potassium and renal function 1-2 weeks after starting and with each dose change.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','hard','mcq','A 60-year-old woman with HFrEF (EF 25%) has sinus rhythm, QRS duration 155 ms with LBBB morphology, and NYHA Class III symptoms despite optimal medical therapy. Which device therapy is most appropriate?','["A. Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) alone", "B. Permanent pacemaker with rate-responsive pacing", "C. Mechanical ventricular assist device (LVAD)", "D. Cardiac resynchronisation therapy with defibrillator (CRT-D)"]','D','Cardiac resynchronisation therapy (CRT) is indicated in HFrEF patients with EF ≤35%, NYHA Class II-IV symptoms despite optimal medical therapy, and QRS ≥130 ms especially with LBBB morphology. This patient meets all three criteria (EF 25%, NYHA III, QRS 155 ms LBBB). CRT-D (combined CRT and ICD) is preferred as she also qualifies for ICD with EF less than 35% and NYHA II-IV on optimal therapy. ICD alone (option A) would not address dyssynchrony. Permanent pacemaker (option B) does not improve cardiac function in HFrEF. LVAD (option D) is reserved for advanced refractory HF or as a bridge to transplantation. Clinical pearl: CRT improves LBBB-induced dyssynchrony by coordinating septal and lateral wall contraction, improving EF by 10-15%, reducing hospitalisation, and improving survival. LBBB with QRS greater than 150 ms has the greatest treatment response.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','hard','mcq','A 68-year-old man is admitted with acute decompensated heart failure. Despite IV furosemide and oxygen, his BP is 78/50 mmHg and cardiac output is severely reduced. Which inotrope does Ghana STG recommend for this cardiogenic shock?','["A. Digoxin IV 0.5 mg", "B. Dobutamine IV infusion 2.5-10 mcg/kg/min", "C. Adrenaline IV infusion 0.5 mcg/kg/min", "D. Noradrenaline IV infusion"]','B','Per Ghana STG, dobutamine is the inotrope of choice for cardiogenic shock complicating heart failure. Dobutamine acts on beta-1 receptors to increase cardiac contractility and output, and beta-2 receptors to reduce systemic vascular resistance (afterload), making it ideal for low-output states. Digoxin IV (option A) provides only mild positive inotropic effect and is not suitable for acute cardiogenic shock. Adrenaline (option B) has high arrhythmogenic risk and increases myocardial oxygen demand significantly. It is a last resort in refractory shock. Noradrenaline (option C) increases afterload through alpha-1 agonism, which worsens cardiac function in pure cardiogenic shock. It is used only for vasodilatory shock or when MAP is dangerously low despite dobutamine. Clinical pearl: Dobutamine is the preferred inotrope for cardiogenic shock because it increases CO while reducing afterload. However it has no vasopressor effect, so profound hypotension with SBP below 70 mmHg may require concomitant noradrenaline or dopamine.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','hard','mcq','A 75-year-old man with HFrEF (EF 30%) develops fast atrial fibrillation with ventricular rate 148 bpm and worsening dyspnoea. His BP is 95/60 mmHg. Which is the most appropriate rate control agent?','["A. Digoxin 0.5 mg IV over 30 minutes", "B. Verapamil 5 mg IV", "C. Bisoprolol 5 mg orally", "D. Amiodarone 300 mg IV over 20 minutes"]','A','In fast AF complicating decompensated HFrEF with low BP (95/60 mmHg), digoxin is the preferred rate control agent. Digoxin slows AV nodal conduction without significant negative inotropy, making it safe in patients with low EF and low BP. Per Ghana STG, digoxin is recommended for rate control in AF with heart failure when BP is low. Beta-blockers (bisoprolol, option B) should be avoided or used with extreme caution in acute decompensated HF. Verapamil (option A) has significant negative inotropy and is absolutely contraindicated in severe systolic dysfunction. Amiodarone (option C) can be used if rhythm control is attempted, but in acute decompensated HF with borderline BP, rate control with digoxin is safer. Clinical pearl: Digoxin is the only rate-controlling agent safe in AF with severe systolic dysfunction and haemodynamic compromise. All other rate control agents including beta-blockers and non-dihydropyridine CCBs are negative inotropes that can worsen cardiogenic shock.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old woman with HFpEF (EF 60%) presents with exertional dyspnoea and ankle oedema. Echo shows impaired relaxation with E/A ratio 0.7 and elevated LV filling pressures. Which statement best describes management?','["A. Diuretics for symptom relief, treat underlying hypertension, and manage risk factors as no mortality-reducing therapy is proven for HFpEF", "B. Start carvedilol immediately as all heart failure responds to beta-blockers", "C. Start high-dose ACE inhibitor and beta-blocker to improve ejection fraction", "D. Add digoxin to improve diastolic function"]','A','HFpEF (heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, EF ≥50%) management focuses on symptom relief and treating underlying causes such as hypertension, AF, obesity, and diabetes, as no therapy has been proven to reduce mortality in HFpEF. Diuretics relieve congestion symptoms. The treatments proven in HFrEF including ACE inhibitors, beta-blockers, and spironolactone have not shown mortality benefits in HFpEF randomised trials. SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin) showed benefit in recent trials but are not yet standard in Ghana STG. HFpEF cannot be improved to higher EF with ACE-I and beta-blockers as the EF is already preserved (option C). Digoxin (option D) does not improve diastolic function. Clinical pearl: HFpEF vs HFrEF distinction is critical. HFpEF has abnormal relaxation (diastolic dysfunction) with normal EF, while HFrEF has reduced EF. Treatments differ. Always confirm EF on echocardiogram before prescribing HF mortality-reducing therapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','easy','mcq','Atrial fibrillation that terminates spontaneously within 7 days and may recur is classified as:','["A. Persistent AF", "B. Permanent AF", "C. Long-standing persistent AF", "D. Paroxysmal AF"]','D','Paroxysmal AF self-terminates within 7 days (usually within 48 hours) and may recur. Persistent AF lasts more than 7 days or requires cardioversion to restore sinus rhythm. Long-standing persistent AF has lasted continuously for more than 12 months. Permanent AF refers to AF where sinus rhythm restoration is no longer attempted, which is accepted by both patient and physician. The classification guides treatment. Paroxysmal AF can often be managed with rate control and anticoagulation alone, while persistent AF may require cardioversion or rhythm control strategy. Clinical pearl: The classification of AF is not static. Paroxysmal AF can evolve to persistent and then to long-standing persistent as left atrial remodelling progresses with untreated or undertreated AF.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','easy','mcq','Which ECG finding is characteristic of Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome?','["A. Prolonged QT interval >500 ms", "B. Short PR interval (<120 ms) with a delta wave and widened QRS", "C. ST elevation in V1-V3 with right bundle branch block pattern", "D. Peaked P waves in lead II and V1 (P pulmonale)"]','B','WPW syndrome is caused by an accessory pathway (bundle of Kent) bypassing the AV node, causing ventricular pre-excitation. The characteristic ECG shows: short PR interval less than 120 ms as the impulse bypasses AV node delay, a delta wave which is a slurred QRS upstroke due to slow accessory pathway conduction, and widened QRS. Prolonged QT (option A) is seen in long QT syndrome or drug effects. Brugada pattern (option C) shows coved ST elevation with RBBB morphology in V1-V3 but is a different channelopathy. Peaked P waves (option D) indicate right atrial enlargement (P pulmonale). Clinical pearl: WPW with AF is life-threatening. Rapid conduction down the accessory pathway can cause rates above 300 bpm and degenerate into VF. AV nodal blocking agents including digoxin, verapamil, and adenosine are CONTRAINDICATED in WPW with AF.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','easy','mcq','In first-degree AV block, which ECG finding is characteristic?','["A. Progressive lengthening of PR interval until a QRS is dropped (Wenckebach)", "B. Fixed prolonged PR interval >200 ms with all P waves conducted to the ventricle", "C. Complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes", "D. Alternating conducted and non-conducted P waves in a fixed 2:1 ratio"]','B','First-degree AV block is characterised by a fixed, prolonged PR interval greater than 200 ms (5 small squares) with all P waves being conducted to the ventricles, meaning there are no dropped beats. It represents a conduction delay in the AV node rather than a true block. Progressive PR lengthening until a QRS is dropped (option A) describes Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach) second-degree AV block. Complete P-QRS dissociation (option C) describes complete (third-degree) heart block. Fixed 2:1 ratio (option D) describes Mobitz Type II second-degree AV block. Clinical pearl: First-degree AV block alone rarely requires treatment unless the PR is extremely prolonged causing symptoms from loss of atrial kick. It can progress to higher-degree blocks, particularly after inferior MI affecting the AV nodal artery.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','medium','mcq','A 68-year-old man with persistent AF and a CHA2DS2-VASc score of 4 is being managed for rate control. What is the recommended stroke prevention therapy?','["A. Aspirin 75 mg daily alone", "B. Aspirin 75 mg plus clopidogrel 75 mg daily", "C. No anticoagulation needed - threshold is score of 5 or above", "D. Warfarin with target INR 2-3"]','D','Warfarin with target INR 2.0-3.0 is recommended for stroke prevention in AF with CHA2DS2-VASc ≥2 in men (or ≥3 in women). A score of 4 clearly mandates anticoagulation. Per Ghana STG, warfarin is the available oral anticoagulant for AF stroke prevention. Aspirin alone (option A) provides only modest stroke reduction (22%) compared to warfarin (64%) in AF and is no longer recommended for stroke prevention in AF. Dual antiplatelet therapy (option B) has higher bleeding risk than warfarin with inferior stroke prevention efficacy in AF. A score of 4 clearly warrants anticoagulation (option C is incorrect). Clinical pearl: The CHA2DS2-VASc score assigns points for: Congestive HF (1), Hypertension (1), Age ≥75 years (2), Diabetes (1), Stroke or TIA history (2), Vascular disease (1), Age 65-74 (1), and Sex category female (1). Maximum score is 9.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old woman presents with AF of 36-hour duration and a ventricular rate of 140 bpm. BP is 125/80 mmHg. According to Ghana STG, what is the preferred rate control agent?','["A. Verapamil 5 mg IV followed by oral verapamil", "B. Digoxin 0.5 mg IV over 20-30 minutes", "C. Atenolol 25-50 mg orally or bisoprolol 2.5-5 mg orally", "D. Amiodarone 300 mg IV over 60 minutes"]','C','Per Ghana STG, beta-blockers (atenolol, bisoprolol, metoprolol) are the preferred first-line rate control agents for AF when the patient is haemodynamically stable. They rapidly reduce ventricular rate by slowing AV nodal conduction. Verapamil (option A) is an alternative to beta-blockers but MUST NOT be combined with them due to the risk of complete heart block and asystole from additive AV nodal depression. Digoxin (option C) is a second-line rate control agent per Ghana STG. It is slower acting and less effective at controlling rate during exertion. It is used when beta-blockers are contraindicated. Amiodarone (option D) is a rhythm control agent and antiarrhythmic, not first-line for rate control. Clinical pearl: Rate control target in AF is resting heart rate below 110 bpm for most patients (lenient approach), and below 80 bpm if symptoms persist (strict approach). Never combine beta-blockers and verapamil or diltiazem for rate control due to the risk of complete heart block.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','medium','mcq','A 60-year-old man with AF has been in atrial fibrillation for 5 days. The decision is made to attempt DC cardioversion. What must be ensured before elective cardioversion?','["A. The patient must have had AF for less than 48 hours only - this patient is not eligible", "B. Thyroid function tests must be normal before any cardioversion", "C. Either ≥3 weeks of adequate anticoagulation (INR 2-3) OR TOE showing no left atrial thrombus", "D. Coronary angiography must be performed first to exclude underlying CAD"]','C','Before elective DC cardioversion of AF lasting more than 48 hours (or unknown duration), either: (1) a minimum of 3 weeks of therapeutic anticoagulation (INR 2-3 on warfarin) to allow any existing left atrial thrombus to resolve, OR (2) a trans-oesophageal echocardiogram (TOE) to confirm the absence of left atrial or LAA thrombus, followed by immediate cardioversion and continuation of anticoagulation for at least 4 weeks post-cardioversion. This patient has had AF for 5 days (more than 48 hours), making anticoagulation mandatory before cardioversion. Option A incorrectly states the patient is not eligible. Thyroid function (option B) should be checked as a cause of AF but does not determine cardioversion timing. Coronary angiography (option D) is not required before cardioversion. Clinical pearl: The 48-hour rule states that AF less than 48 hours can be cardioverted with just heparin cover, while AF 48 hours or longer requires 3 weeks prior anticoagulation or TOE to exclude thrombus.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','hard','mcq','A 40-year-old man with known WPW syndrome develops rapid irregular palpitations. ECG shows rates of 220-280 bpm with irregular, wide, bizarre QRS complexes of varying morphology. Which treatment is MOST appropriate?','["A. IV adenosine 6 mg bolus", "B. IV digoxin 0.5 mg over 30 minutes", "C. IV verapamil 5 mg", "D. Synchronised DC cardioversion"]','D','This patient has pre-excited AF (WPW plus AF) with extremely rapid ventricular rates of 220-280 bpm, which is a life-threatening emergency. The irregular wide-complex tachycardia with variable QRS morphology is the hallmark of AF conducting rapidly down an accessory pathway. DC cardioversion is the treatment of choice for haemodynamically significant pre-excited AF. All AV nodal blocking agents including adenosine (option A), digoxin (option B), verapamil (option C), and beta-blockers are absolutely CONTRAINDICATED in WPW with AF. These agents block the normal AV node pathway, forcing all conduction down the accessory pathway, potentially causing rates above 300 bpm and degeneration into ventricular fibrillation and death. Clinical pearl: The three drugs that can kill in WPW with AF are verapamil, digoxin, and adenosine. Remember: WIDE plus IRREGULAR plus FAST equals pre-excited AF. Do NOT use AV nodal blockers.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','hard','mcq','A 65-year-old woman develops a prolonged QTc of 560 ms on sotalol. She then has an episode of polymorphic VT with a twisting-axis pattern. What is the most appropriate immediate treatment?','["A. Synchronised DC cardioversion and increase sotalol dose", "B. IV magnesium sulphate 2 g over 10-15 minutes and stop the offending drug", "C. IV adenosine 6 mg bolus to terminate the arrhythmia", "D. IV amiodarone 300 mg over 20 minutes as the first-line antiarrhythmic"]','B','This is torsades de pointes (TdP), a polymorphic VT occurring in the setting of prolonged QT, characterised by the twisting of QRS complexes around the isoelectric line. Immediate management: IV magnesium sulphate 2 g over 10-15 minutes (even if serum magnesium is normal) and STOP the offending drug (sotalol is a class III antiarrhythmic causing QT prolongation). Temporary pacing or isoprenaline can be used to shorten the QT interval by increasing heart rate. Sotalol must not be increased (option A) as it is the cause of the QT prolongation. Adenosine (option B) has no role in VT. Amiodarone (option D) also prolongs QT and is contraindicated in TdP as it would worsen the arrhythmia. Clinical pearl: Causes of long QT and TdP include medications (antiarrhythmics, antibiotics, antipsychotics), electrolyte abnormalities (low K+, Mg2+, Ca2+), hereditary LQTS, and bradycardia. Magnesium is the immediate treatment regardless of serum magnesium level.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','hard','mcq','A 70-year-old man in the ICU develops ventricular fibrillation after an anterior MI. Immediate CPR is commenced. Which is the correct sequence of management?','["A. Defibrillate 200 J (biphasic), resume CPR for 2 minutes, give adrenaline 1 mg IV every 3-5 minutes, amiodarone 300 mg IV after 3rd shock if VF persists", "B. Adrenaline 1 mg IV then amiodarone 300 mg IV first, then defibrillate once at 200 J", "C. Lignocaine 100 mg IV bolus as first-line antiarrhythmic before any defibrillation", "D. Defibrillate at 100 J first, then give digoxin IV if VF persists"]','A','Ventricular fibrillation management follows Advanced Life Support protocol: (1) Call for defibrillator while performing CPR, (2) Defibrillate 200 J biphasic immediately when available, (3) Resume CPR immediately for 2 minutes, (4) If VF persists: give adrenaline 1 mg IV and defibrillate again. Adrenaline is repeated every 3-5 minutes. (5) After the 3rd shock, give amiodarone 300 mg IV bolus as the preferred antiarrhythmic. Option A incorrectly delays defibrillation. Lignocaine (option C) is a second-line antiarrhythmic used only if amiodarone is unavailable. Option D uses incorrect energy (100 J too low for VF) and digoxin, which is inappropriate in VF. Clinical pearl: In VF, defibrillation is the only definitive treatment. Do not delay it. CPR buys time by maintaining coronary and cerebral perfusion but does not terminate VF. The energy of first shock for VF should be the maximum dose for the defibrillator used (usually 200 J biphasic).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','hard','mcq','A 72-year-old man on digoxin for heart failure with AF develops a regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 150 bpm. Flutter waves at 300 bpm are visible in lead II. The clinician considers the risk of digoxin in this arrhythmia. What is the primary concern?','["A. Digoxin can convert 2:1 flutter to 1:1 flutter, doubling the ventricular rate to 300 bpm", "B. Digoxin is safe and should be increased to control the ventricular rate", "C. Digoxin always converts atrial flutter to sinus rhythm", "D. Digoxin is the preferred agent as it has no effect on AV conduction in flutter"]','A','Atrial flutter characteristically has an atrial rate of 250-350 bpm with AV block (usually 2:1 giving ventricular rate approximately 150 bpm). Digoxin slows AV conduction and can improve the degree of AV block (from 2:1 to 4:1), but paradoxically, by increasing vagal tone it can enhance accessory pathway conduction or unpredictably change AV block ratio. The primary danger is that digoxin can occasionally convert 2:1 flutter to 1:1 conduction by reducing atrial rate slightly while vagal effects wear off, leading to rates of 250-300 bpm and haemodynamic collapse. Increasing digoxin (option A) is inappropriate without cardioversion or rate control reassessment. Digoxin does not reliably convert flutter to sinus rhythm (option C). Digoxin does affect AV conduction in flutter (option D is incorrect). Clinical pearl: Atrial flutter with 2:1 block presenting as regular tachycardia at 150 bpm is the classic presentation. Vagal manoeuvres transiently slow rate to 75 bpm (4:1) revealing flutter waves. DC cardioversion is the preferred treatment for flutter.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','easy','mcq','A 65-year-old man has a harsh ejection systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotids. Carotid upstroke is slow-rising and diminished. What is the most likely valvular diagnosis?','["A. Mitral regurgitation", "B. Aortic regurgitation", "C. Aortic stenosis", "D. Pulmonary stenosis"]','C','Aortic stenosis (AS) produces a harsh mid-systolic (ejection systolic) murmur heard best at the right second intercostal space (aortic area) radiating to the carotids. The pulse is slow-rising (pulsus parvus et tardus), reflecting reduced stroke volume through the stenotic valve. Other signs include a soft or absent A2 (aortic component of S2), sustained apex beat indicating LV hypertrophy, and a systolic thrill at the base. Mitral regurgitation (option B) causes a holosystolic murmur best heard at the apex radiating to the axilla. Aortic regurgitation (option C) causes an early diastolic murmur at the left sternal border and a collapsing (waterhammer) pulse. Pulmonary stenosis (option D) is heard best at the left upper sternal border. Clinical pearl: The classic symptomatic triad of severe AS is Angina, Syncope, and heart failure (Dyspnoea). Mean survival after onset of each symptom is 5, 3, and 2 years respectively.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','easy','mcq','Which murmur is classically associated with rheumatic mitral stenosis?','["A. Mid-systolic click followed by late systolic murmur", "B. Opening snap followed by a low-pitched mid-diastolic rumble at the apex", "C. High-pitched early diastolic murmur at the left sternal border", "D. Continuous machinery murmur at the left infraclavicular area"]','B','Mitral stenosis (MS) produces a low-pitched mid-diastolic rumbling murmur heard best at the apex in the left lateral decubitus position with the bell of the stethoscope. It is preceded by an opening snap (OS). The shorter the S2-OS interval, the more severe the stenosis as high left atrial pressure opens the mitral valve sooner. The murmur may have presystolic accentuation if sinus rhythm is maintained. Option A (mid-systolic click plus late systolic murmur) is mitral valve prolapse. Option C (high-pitched early diastolic murmur at left sternal border) is aortic regurgitation. Option D (continuous machinery murmur) is patent ductus arteriosus. Clinical pearl: In MS the murmur INTENSIFIES with exercise due to increased flow and may be absent when stenosis is very severe due to markedly reduced flow. Always auscultate in the left lateral decubitus position with the bell of the stethoscope.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','easy','mcq','Which of the following is the most common cause of mitral regurgitation in sub-Saharan Africa?','["A. Rheumatic heart disease", "B. Mitral valve prolapse", "C. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy", "D. Marfan syndrome"]','A','Rheumatic heart disease (RHD) is the most common cause of mitral regurgitation and mitral stenosis in sub-Saharan Africa and other developing regions, resulting from repeated streptococcal pharyngitis leading to rheumatic fever and valvular scarring. RHD remains a major cause of cardiac morbidity and mortality in Africa, particularly affecting young adults aged 20-40 years. Mitral valve prolapse (option A) is the most common cause of MR in developed countries. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (option C) can cause functional MR from systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve but is a rare cause. Marfan syndrome (option D) primarily affects the aortic root and can cause MV prolapse but is uncommon. Clinical pearl: Secondary prevention of RHD requires monthly benzathine penicillin G 1.2 million units IM for at least 10 years or until age 21 (whichever is longer), or lifelong if severe valvular disease is present.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old man with severe AS (aortic valve area 0.7 cm2, mean gradient 52 mmHg) has developed exertional dyspnoea and syncope. His LV ejection fraction is 55%. What is the most appropriate management?','["A. Medical therapy with ACE inhibitor and beta-blocker, repeat echo in 6 months", "B. Balloon aortic valvuloplasty as definitive treatment", "C. Start furosemide and restrict fluids only", "D. Aortic valve replacement (surgical AVR or TAVR)"]','D','Symptomatic severe AS (aortic valve area <1.0 cm2 with mean gradient >40 mmHg) with symptoms (dyspnoea AND syncope in this case) is a Class I indication for aortic valve replacement. Surgical AVR or TAVR for high-risk surgical patients is the only definitive treatment that improves survival. Medical therapy (option A) does not improve outcomes in symptomatic severe AS. ACE inhibitors must be used cautiously as they can cause hypotension. Balloon valvuloplasty (option C) provides only temporary relief as restenosis occurs within 6-12 months. It is used as a bridge to definitive intervention. Diuretics alone (option D) may worsen preload-dependent haemodynamics in severe AS. Clinical pearl: Symptomatic AS carries a very poor prognosis without intervention. Once symptoms develop, 50% die within 2-3 years without valve replacement. Asymptomatic severe AS is managed conservatively with close echocardiographic follow-up.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old woman from Ghana with known rheumatic mitral stenosis develops AF with rapid ventricular rate. Which is the most important management consideration?','["A. Immediate cardioversion without anticoagulation as mitral stenosis is the underlying cause", "B. Start dual antiplatelet therapy and proceed with cardioversion", "C. Anticoagulate with warfarin (INR 2.5-3.5) and achieve rate control before any cardioversion", "D. Start spironolactone and amlodipine for rate control in this valvular condition"]','C','Rheumatic mitral stenosis with AF carries a very high thromboembolic risk due to left atrial enlargement and sluggish blood flow with increased risk of LA thrombus. Anticoagulation with warfarin targeting INR 2.5-3.5 (higher target than non-valvular AF INR 2-3) is mandatory before any cardioversion attempt. Rate control with beta-blockers or digoxin is achieved while anticoagulation is being established. Cardioversion without anticoagulation (option A) risks dislodging LA thrombus causing embolic stroke. Dual antiplatelet therapy (option C) is inadequate for valvular AF in rheumatic MS. Warfarin is required. Spironolactone and amlodipine (option D) are not appropriate for AF rate control in this clinical context. Clinical pearl: Mitral stenosis with AF is one of the highest-risk thromboembolic states in cardiology. Always anticoagulate with warfarin as direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs) are NOT approved for AF in the context of valvular disease from rheumatic mitral stenosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','medium','mcq','A 30-year-old woman is found to have an early diastolic murmur at the left sternal border. Echo shows severe aortic regurgitation with LV end-systolic diameter 52 mm and EF 48%. She is asymptomatic. What is the management?','["A. Watchful waiting only - intervene only when she develops symptoms", "B. Start ACE inhibitor to reduce afterload and monitor with serial echo", "C. Aortic valve replacement is indicated based on LV dimensions and reduced EF", "D. Start warfarin anticoagulation and digoxin"]','C','Aortic valve replacement is indicated in asymptomatic severe AR when there is LV dysfunction (EF <50%) OR LV dilatation (end-systolic diameter >50 mm). This patient has BOTH reduced EF (48%, below the 50% threshold) and borderline LVESD (52 mm, above the 50 mm threshold), meeting surgical criteria even without symptoms. Waiting for symptoms alone (option A) risks irreversible LV damage as the heart has already begun to fail. ACE inhibitors (option B) reduce afterload and can be used as a bridge to surgery or in those who refuse surgery, but they do not replace the need for intervention when surgical criteria are met. Warfarin and digoxin (option D) have no role in isolated AR management without other indications. Clinical pearl: In AR, surgery is not always delayed until symptoms develop. Echocardiographic criteria of EF <50% or LVESD >50 mm trigger intervention to prevent permanent LV dysfunction and improve long-term post-operative outcomes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','hard','mcq','A 25-year-old man presents with fever, fatigue, and a new murmur 6 weeks after dental extraction. Echo shows a bicuspid aortic valve with a 1.5 cm vegetation and moderate aortic regurgitation. Which finding is an indication for urgent surgery?','["A. Patient age under 30 years with bicuspid aortic valve", "B. Positive blood cultures after only 3 days of antibiotics", "C. ESR elevation above 50 mm per hour", "D. Vegetation size greater than 1 cm with haemodynamic instability or refractory infection"]','D','Surgical indications in infective endocarditis include: (1) Heart failure due to valvular regurgitation or obstruction not responding to medical management, (2) Uncontrolled infection such as persistent bacteraemia after 7-10 days of antibiotics or perivalvular extension and abscess, and (3) Prevention of embolism - mobile vegetation greater than 10 mm, particularly on the aortic or mitral valve, causing recurrent emboli. This patient has a 15 mm vegetation with haemodynamic compromise (moderate AR), meeting multiple criteria for surgery. Age (option A) is not a surgical indication. Positive cultures at 3 days (option C) is expected and does not indicate surgical failure - persistence after 7-10 days indicates uncontrolled infection. Elevated ESR (option D) is a nonspecific inflammatory marker without surgical implication. Clinical pearl: Aortic valve IE has a higher surgical rate than mitral valve IE because aortic regurgitation causes acute haemodynamic deterioration as the LV has less time to adapt compared with chronic regurgitation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','hard','mcq','A 60-year-old man with a mechanical prosthetic mitral valve (replaced 8 years ago) develops fever and a new regurgitant murmur. Blood cultures grow Staphylococcus epidermidis. This is classified as:','["A. Native valve endocarditis community-acquired", "B. Late prosthetic valve endocarditis (>12 months after surgery) associated with coagulase-negative staphylococci", "C. Early prosthetic valve endocarditis (<2 months after surgery) associated with Staph aureus", "D. Culture-negative endocarditis from HACEK organisms"]','B','Prosthetic valve endocarditis (PVE) is classified as early (less than 2 months post-op) or late (more than 12 months after surgery). Late PVE occurs more than 12 months after surgery and is caused by community-acquired organisms similar to native valve IE, including Streptococcus viridans and coagulase-negative staphylococci such as S. epidermidis. This patient is 8 years post-surgery making this late PVE. Early PVE (option C) occurring less than 2 months post-surgery is caused by organisms introduced at surgery: S. aureus, S. epidermidis, gram-negative bacilli, and fungi. Option A is incorrect as this is a prosthetic valve. HACEK organisms (option D) cause culture-negative IE in native valves after dental procedures. They are fastidious gram-negative organisms, not coagulase-negative staphylococci. Clinical pearl: Late PVE is managed like native valve IE but with longer antibiotic courses of 6 weeks. Early PVE almost always requires surgery due to high mortality with antibiotics alone.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old woman has severe symptomatic mitral stenosis (MVA 0.8 cm2) with favourable valve morphology (Wilkins score 6). She has no LA thrombus on TOE and is in sinus rhythm. What is the preferred intervention?','["A. Percutaneous mitral balloon commissurotomy (PMBC)", "B. Surgical mitral valve replacement with a mechanical prosthesis", "C. Surgical open mitral commissurotomy", "D. Medical management with diuretics and rate control only"]','A','Percutaneous mitral balloon commissurotomy (PMBC) is the treatment of choice for symptomatic severe mitral stenosis with favourable valve morphology (Wilkins score ≤8, indicating pliable leaflets, minimal calcification, minimal subvalvular disease, and minimal MR). The favourable features in this patient (Wilkins score 6, no LA thrombus, sinus rhythm, no significant MR) make PMBC the preferred option with outcomes comparable to surgical commissurotomy. Surgical MVR (option A) is reserved for unfavourable anatomy such as heavily calcified valves or significant MR, or failed PMBC. Surgical commissurotomy (option B) is an open alternative but carries higher perioperative risk than PMBC for suitable candidates. Medical management (option D) is inadequate for symptomatic severe MS. Clinical pearl: The Wilkins score assesses mitral valve suitability for PMBC across four parameters: Leaflet mobility, Leaflet thickening, Calcification, and Subvalvular involvement, each scored 0-4. A total score of 8 or less predicts a good PMBC outcome.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','hard','mcq','A 35-year-old woman with Marfan syndrome has an aortic root diameter of 50 mm and moderate aortic regurgitation but no symptoms. What is the recommended management?','["A. Start a beta-blocker and consider prophylactic aortic root surgery at this dimension", "B. Reassure and review annually as she is asymptomatic", "C. Start warfarin and aspirin for thromboembolic protection", "D. Await development of symptoms before any surgical intervention"]','A','In Marfan syndrome, prophylactic aortic root surgery (replacement) is recommended when the aortic root diameter reaches 45-50 mm, or earlier if there is rapid expansion greater than 2 mm per year, family history of aortic dissection, or planned pregnancy. This patient has a 50 mm root, meeting the threshold for surgery. Beta-blockers and ARBs (losartan) are standard medical therapy to reduce the rate of aortic root expansion by lowering shear stress. Annual surveillance alone (option A) is inadequate at this dimension as the risk of dissection rises significantly above 50 mm. Warfarin and aspirin (option C) have no role in uncomplicated Marfan aortic disease without a prosthetic valve. Awaiting symptoms (option D) risks Type A aortic dissection before intervention. Clinical pearl: In Marfan syndrome the aortic root is weakened by abnormal fibrillin-1. Surgery is preventive, not reactive. The threshold for surgery is lower than for degenerative aortic disease (50 mm versus 55 mm) due to higher dissection risk at lower diameters.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','easy','mcq','Which of the following is a MAJOR Duke criterion for infective endocarditis?','["A. Fever >38°C", "B. Predisposing cardiac condition", "C. Vascular phenomena such as Janeway lesions", "D. Positive blood culture with a typical organism (e.g., Strep viridans) from two separate blood cultures"]','D','Major Duke criteria include: (1) Positive blood cultures with typical IE organisms (Strep viridans, Strep gallolyticus, HACEK group, Staph aureus, or Enterococcus spp.) from two separate blood cultures, or persistently positive blood cultures, and (2) Evidence of endocardial involvement such as a positive echocardiogram showing vegetation, abscess, or new dehiscence of a prosthetic valve, or new valvular regurgitation. Fever greater than 38°C (option A), predisposing cardiac condition (option C), and vascular phenomena such as Janeway lesions (option D) are all MINOR criteria. Clinical pearl: Modified Duke criteria diagnose IE as follows: Definite IE requires 2 major criteria, OR 1 major plus 3 minor criteria, OR 5 minor criteria. Pathological criteria including positive histology or culture from a vegetation are also definitive.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','easy','mcq','Which organism is most commonly responsible for native valve infective endocarditis in community settings (non-IV drug user)?','["A. Staphylococcus epidermidis", "B. Streptococcus viridans", "C. Enterococcus faecalis", "D. Candida albicans"]','B','Streptococcus viridans (alpha-haemolytic streptococci including S. sanguis, S. mutans, and S. mitis) is the most common cause of native valve infective endocarditis in community settings, typically following dental procedures. It is highly sensitive to penicillin. Staphylococcus epidermidis (option A) is the most common cause of prosthetic valve IE (early, less than 2 months post-surgery) and hospital-acquired IE. Enterococcus faecalis (option C) is associated with gastrointestinal or genitourinary procedures. Candida albicans (option D) causes fungal IE, typically in IV drug users or immunocompromised patients. Clinical pearl: In IV drug users, Staphylococcus aureus is the most common IE organism rather than Strep viridans. It commonly affects the tricuspid valve (right-sided IE), causing septic pulmonary emboli that appear as multiple nodular infiltrates on chest X-ray.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','easy','mcq','Which cardiac condition carries the HIGHEST risk and requires antibiotic prophylaxis before high-risk dental procedures?','["A. Bicuspid aortic valve", "B. Previous infective endocarditis or prosthetic heart valve", "C. Mitral valve prolapse with mild regurgitation", "D. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"]','B','The highest-risk cardiac conditions requiring IE prophylaxis before high-risk dental procedures are: (1) Prosthetic heart valves including transcatheter, (2) Previous infective endocarditis, (3) Congenital cyanotic heart disease that is unrepaired or repaired with residual defects or prosthetic material, and (4) Cardiac transplant recipients with valvulopathy. Bicuspid aortic valve (option A) and MVP with MR (option B) are no longer included in prophylaxis guidelines per ESC 2023 revisions. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (option D) is not a prophylaxis indication. The prophylaxis regimen is amoxicillin 2 g orally 30-60 minutes before the dental procedure, or clindamycin 600 mg if penicillin-allergic. Clinical pearl: Most cases of IE result from everyday bacteraemia from routine activities like tooth brushing, not from discrete dental procedures. Prophylaxis targets only the highest-risk structural conditions.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old man with rheumatic mitral regurgitation presents with 3 weeks of fever, fatigue, and weight loss. Examination reveals splinter haemorrhages, Osler nodes, and a new systolic murmur. Blood cultures grow Streptococcus viridans. Echo shows a 0.8 cm mobile vegetation on the mitral valve. What is the treatment?','["A. Oral amoxicillin 500 mg three times daily for 4 weeks", "B. IV vancomycin 1 g every 12 hours for 2 weeks only", "C. Await antibiotic sensitivity results before starting antibiotics", "D. IV benzylpenicillin 1.2-2.4 g every 4 hours for 4 weeks (or ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily for 4 weeks)"]','D','Native valve IE due to penicillin-sensitive Strep viridans is treated with IV benzylpenicillin 1.2-2.4 g every 4 hours for 4 weeks, or ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily for 4 weeks as an alternative suitable for outpatient therapy. Oral amoxicillin (option A) is inadequate for IE as parenteral therapy is required to achieve bactericidal serum levels at the vegetation. Vancomycin (option C) is reserved for penicillin-allergic patients or MRSA. Two weeks is too short a course. Delaying antibiotics while awaiting culture sensitivities (option D) is inappropriate in an acutely unwell patient with clinical IE. Three sets of blood cultures are drawn then empirical antibiotics are started immediately. Clinical pearl: For uncomplicated Strep viridans IE, a 4-week course of IV penicillin or ceftriaxone achieves cure rates above 95%. Gentamicin synergy can be added for the first 2 weeks in uncomplicated cases to potentially shorten treatment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','medium','mcq','A 28-year-old IV drug user presents with fever, cough, and pleuritic chest pain. CXR shows bilateral nodular infiltrates. Echo shows a vegetation on the tricuspid valve. Blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. What is the treatment?','["A. IV benzylpenicillin for 4 weeks", "B. Oral doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 6 weeks", "C. IV flucloxacillin 2 g every 4-6 hours for 4-6 weeks", "D. IV ceftriaxone 2 g daily for 2 weeks"]','C','Right-sided (tricuspid valve) IE due to methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus (MSSA) in an IV drug user is treated with IV flucloxacillin (or nafcillin) 2 g every 4-6 hours for 4-6 weeks. The bilateral nodular infiltrates represent septic pulmonary emboli from tricuspid valve vegetations. Benzylpenicillin (option A) does not cover S. aureus as it is a penicillinase (beta-lactamase) producer. Oral doxycycline (option C) is inadequate for S. aureus IE as parenteral bactericidal concentrations are required. Ceftriaxone (option D) is not appropriate for S. aureus IE. Clinical pearl: If MRSA is suspected due to known MRSA colonisation, healthcare exposure, or treatment failure, vancomycin 1 g every 12 hours targeting a trough of 15-20 mg/L or daptomycin replace flucloxacillin. Always check MRSA status before finalising therapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','medium','mcq','HACEK organisms are a recognised cause of culture-negative infective endocarditis. Which of the following is a member of the HACEK group?','["A. Haemophilus influenzae type b", "B. Histoplasma capsulatum", "C. Haemophilus parainfluenzae, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella", "D. Helicobacter pylori"]','C','HACEK stands for: Haemophilus parainfluenzae and aphrophilus, Aggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans, Cardiobacterium hominis, Eikenella corrodens, and Kingella kingae. These are slow-growing, fastidious gram-negative oral flora that can cause indolent endocarditis, often presenting with large vegetations and embolic complications. They classically cause culture-negative endocarditis with routine 5-day incubation as they require 14 or more days to grow. Haemophilus influenzae type b (option A) is not a HACEK organism per modern classification. Histoplasma (option C) causes fungal IE. H. pylori (option D) is a gastrointestinal pathogen. Treatment for HACEK IE is IV ceftriaxone 2 g daily for 4 weeks. Clinical pearl: HACEK IE should be suspected in subacute IE with large vegetations and negative blood cultures after 5 days. Inform the microbiology lab to extend culture incubation to 14 days and consider 16S rRNA PCR on valve tissue for diagnosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','hard','mcq','A 70-year-old man with a bioprosthetic aortic valve replacement (18 months ago) presents with fever, weight loss, and a new perivalvular regurgitant murmur. TOE shows a 1.2 cm vegetation and perivalvular abscess. Blood cultures grow methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus epidermidis (MRSE). What is the management?','["A. IV vancomycin 1 g every 12 hours for 6 weeks alone", "B. Oral linezolid 600 mg twice daily for 4 weeks", "C. IV teicoplanin + ceftriaxone for 4 weeks, defer surgery", "D. IV vancomycin + rifampicin 300-450 mg BD + gentamicin for at least 6 weeks AND early surgical valve replacement"]','D','Late prosthetic valve endocarditis (more than 12 months post-surgery) due to MRSE with perivalvular abscess carries very high mortality with antibiotics alone. Treatment requires: (1) IV vancomycin plus rifampicin (which penetrates biofilm) plus gentamicin for at least 6 weeks, AND (2) Early surgical valve replacement, which is mandatory when a perivalvular abscess is present. Perivalvular abscess, heart failure, persistent bacteraemia, and large vegetations are all indications for surgery in PVE. Vancomycin alone (option A) is insufficient as rifampicin is essential for penetrating the prosthetic valve biofilm. Oral linezolid (option C) is not appropriate for severe PVE with abscess requiring surgical intervention. Deferring surgery (option D) in the presence of perivalvular abscess is associated with treatment failure and death. Clinical pearl: Prosthetic valve IE mortality exceeds 20-40% even with optimal combined treatment. The combination of antibiotics plus surgery reduces mortality compared to antibiotics alone in complicated PVE.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','hard','mcq','A 45-year-old immunocompromised man (post-renal transplant on tacrolimus) develops fever and blood cultures grow Candida albicans. TOE shows a large 2 cm vegetation on the mitral valve. What is the appropriate management?','["A. Fluconazole 400 mg daily for 6 weeks as definitive antifungal therapy", "B. IV micafungin (echinocandin) plus early surgical valve replacement and prolonged antifungal therapy", "C. IV amphotericin B deoxycholate for 4 weeks as sole treatment", "D. IV voriconazole 6 mg/kg every 12 hours for 2 weeks"]','B','Fungal endocarditis carries mortality above 50% and almost universally requires surgery combined with antifungal therapy. Echinocandins (micafungin, caspofungin) are the preferred initial treatment due to their fungicidal activity and favourable safety profile. Surgery is mandatory due to large vegetations (typically greater than 2 cm and highly embolic), poor antifungal penetration of fungal biofilm, and risk of valve destruction. Fluconazole (option A) is fungistatic and insufficient for severe Candida IE. It may be used as long-term suppressive therapy after definitive treatment. Amphotericin B (option B) alone without surgery has poor outcomes for fungal IE and has significant nephrotoxicity, which is a major concern in a renal transplant recipient. Voriconazole (option D) is not first-line for Candida IE. Clinical pearl: Fungal IE red flags are immunocompromised host, large friable vegetations, negative bacterial cultures, IV drug use, or prosthetic valve. Mortality approaches 80-100% without surgery.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old woman with mitral valve IE on IV antibiotics develops sudden left-sided hemiplegia. MRI confirms an ischaemic stroke from cardioembolism. Should anticoagulation be commenced?','["A. Anticoagulation is contraindicated acutely post-embolic stroke in IE. Start warfarin after 2-4 weeks only if a specific indication exists", "B. Start IV heparin immediately to prevent further thromboembolic events", "C. Start dual antiplatelet therapy (aspirin plus clopidogrel) for 3 months", "D. Anticoagulation must begin within 24 hours of any IE-associated embolism"]','A','In IE with ischaemic stroke, anticoagulation is CONTRAINDICATED in the acute period (first 2-4 weeks) due to: the significant risk of haemorrhagic transformation of the infarct, risk of septic aneurysm (mycotic aneurysm) rupture with catastrophic bleeding, and the ongoing bacteraemic state which makes anticoagulation less predictable. The embolic risk from vegetations on antibiotics decreases rapidly in the first two weeks of therapy. If the patient has a mechanical prosthetic valve requiring anticoagulation, the decision must be individualised by a multidisciplinary team, but heparin is generally held for at least 2 weeks post-stroke. Immediate heparin (option A) risks haemorrhagic conversion and mycotic aneurysm rupture. Dual antiplatelet (option C) also increases bleeding risk without sufficient benefit. Option D is incorrect and dangerous. Clinical pearl: In IE, the highest embolic risk is in the FIRST 2 WEEKS of antibiotic therapy. After this, vegetation size decreases and embolic risk falls. Aspirin is not recommended routinely in IE to prevent emboli.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','hard','mcq','Which of the following is an indication for URGENT cardiac surgery in a patient with active infective endocarditis?','["A. Severe aortic regurgitation causing acute pulmonary oedema refractory to medical therapy", "B. Single positive blood culture on day 2 of antibiotic therapy", "C. Vegetation greater than 5 mm in size without other features", "D. ESR greater than 80 mm per hour and elevated CRP"]','A','Urgent surgical indications in IE include: (1) Heart failure due to severe valvular regurgitation or obstruction not responding to medical management, (2) Uncontrolled infection such as persistent bacteraemia after 7-10 days of antibiotics, perivalvular abscess or fistula, and (3) Prevention of embolism with large mobile vegetations greater than 10 mm causing recurrent emboli. Severe AR causing acute pulmonary oedema refractory to medical therapy (option D) is a cardiac surgical emergency. A single positive culture on day 2 (option A) is expected - persistence after 7-10 days indicates uncontrolled infection warranting surgery. Vegetation greater than 5 mm alone (option B) is not sufficient - greater than 10 mm with embolic events is the threshold. Elevated ESR and CRP (option C) are nonspecific inflammatory markers without direct surgical implication. Clinical pearl: The most common reason for early surgery in IE is heart failure due to valvular regurgitation or obstruction, accounting for approximately 50% of IE surgeries. This cannot be managed with antibiotics alone.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','easy','mcq','Beck''s triad is the classic presentation of cardiac tamponade. What are the three components?','["A. Hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular pulse", "B. Tachycardia, pulmonary oedema, and pleuritic chest pain", "C. Hypotension, elevated JVP (distended neck veins), and muffled heart sounds", "D. Fever, pericardial friction rub, and ST elevation"]','C','Beck''s triad of cardiac tamponade consists of: (1) Hypotension from reduced cardiac output due to pericardial compression, (2) Elevated JVP (distended neck veins) from impaired venous return to the compressed heart, and (3) Muffled (distant) heart sounds from the pericardial fluid attenuating cardiac sounds. This triad is present in less than 30% of cases. The fourth hallmark is pulsus paradoxus (greater than 10 mmHg drop in SBP during inspiration). Option A describes Cushing reflex from increased intracranial pressure. Option C describes acute heart failure features, not tamponade. Option D describes pericarditis features but not tamponade physiology. Clinical pearl: Pulsus paradoxus is the most sensitive clinical sign of tamponade. It reflects exaggerated ventricular interdependence due to the fixed pericardial space constraining both ventricles simultaneously.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','easy','mcq','What is the most common cause of acute pericarditis in developed countries?','["A. Tuberculosis", "B. Viral infection (idiopathic or viral)", "C. Bacterial (pyogenic) infection", "D. Uraemia from chronic kidney disease"]','B','Viral infection is the most common cause of acute pericarditis in developed countries, accounting for 80-90% of cases. Common causative viruses include enteroviruses (Coxsackievirus B), Echovirus, EBV, and CMV. In many cases no virus is identified and the aetiology is labelled idiopathic or viral. Tuberculosis (option A) is the most common cause of pericarditis in sub-Saharan Africa and other developing regions. Bacterial pyogenic pericarditis (option B) is uncommon but life-threatening. Uraemic pericarditis (option D) occurs in chronic kidney disease and is an indication for dialysis initiation or intensification. Clinical pearl: In a patient from sub-Saharan Africa presenting with pericarditis, ALWAYS consider tuberculosis as the underlying aetiology. TB pericarditis can progress to constrictive pericarditis if untreated, causing chronic venous congestion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','easy','mcq','What is the most characteristic ECG finding of acute pericarditis?','["A. Widespread concave (saddle-shaped) ST elevation with PR depression in multiple leads", "B. Localised convex ST elevation in V1-V4 only with Q wave formation", "C. Complete heart block with P-QRS dissociation", "D. Symmetrical T-wave inversion in V1-V4 with R wave preservation"]','A','Acute pericarditis produces widespread concave (saddle-shaped or smiley-face) ST elevation affecting most leads reflecting diffuse pericardial inflammation, with PR segment depression which is the most specific finding (seen in 80% of cases) reflecting sub-epicardial atrial injury. This pattern of diffuse ST elevation with PR depression differentiates it from STEMI, which shows convex (dome-shaped) ST elevation in a specific vascular territory with reciprocal ST depression. The ECG changes of pericarditis progress through four stages: Stage I has widespread ST elevation plus PR depression, Stage II has ST normalisation and T wave flattening, Stage III has T-wave inversions, and Stage IV shows normalisation. Complete heart block (option C) is not a pericarditis feature. Anterior T-wave inversions (option D) suggest NSTEMI or PE. Clinical pearl: PR depression is the most SPECIFIC ECG sign of pericarditis. It is most visible in lead II and aVR, where it appears as PR elevation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','medium','mcq','A 25-year-old man presents with 3 days of sharp pleuritic chest pain worse on lying flat and improved by leaning forward. He had an upper respiratory tract infection 2 weeks ago. ECG shows saddle-shaped ST elevation. What is the treatment?','["A. IV labetalol and hospitalisation for 48 hours", "B. Aspirin 600 mg every 8 hours for 2-4 weeks plus colchicine 0.5 mg twice daily for 3 months", "C. Prednisolone 40 mg daily as first-line anti-inflammatory therapy", "D. Oral ibuprofen 400-600 mg three times daily plus colchicine 0.5 mg twice daily for 3 months"]','D','Post-viral acute pericarditis is treated per Ghana STG with ibuprofen (400-600 mg three times daily) plus colchicine (0.5 mg twice daily for 3 months). The combination of NSAIDs plus colchicine significantly reduces the risk of recurrence compared to NSAIDs alone, as demonstrated in the COPE trial. Activity restriction during the symptomatic period is also recommended. Labetalol (option A) has no role in pericarditis. Aspirin at 600 mg every 8 hours (option B) is an alternative to ibuprofen and is preferred in post-MI pericarditis (Dressler syndrome) but ibuprofen is more commonly used for viral pericarditis. Corticosteroids (option D) are NOT first-line. They are reserved for pericarditis refractory to NSAIDs plus colchicine, or for specific aetiologies such as CTD-related or uraemic pericarditis. Steroids increase recurrence risk. Clinical pearl: The 3-month colchicine course is crucial. Stopping early dramatically increases recurrence rates. Colchicine inhibits IL-1beta and neutrophil migration into the pericardium.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','medium','mcq','A 50-year-old man with end-stage renal disease on haemodialysis develops pericarditis with a moderate pericardial effusion. Which management approach differs from viral pericarditis?','["A. Colchicine 0.5 mg twice daily for 3 months is still first-line in renal patients", "B. Ibuprofen 600 mg three times daily should be started immediately", "C. Corticosteroids are preferred over NSAIDs and intensification of dialysis is the key treatment", "D. Aspirin 600 mg every 8 hours for 4 weeks is the preferred NSAID"]','C','Uraemic pericarditis is treated differently from viral pericarditis. NSAIDs are AVOIDED in uraemic pericarditis as they worsen residual renal function and reduce GFR. Colchicine is renally cleared and toxic in severe CKD, so it is also avoided. The primary treatment is INTENSIFICATION OF DIALYSIS - increasing dialysis frequency removes uraemic toxins driving the pericarditis. Corticosteroids (indomethacin-based regimens or oral prednisolone) are used when dialysis intensification fails. Option A is incorrect as colchicine is contraindicated in severe CKD. Options C and D use NSAIDs, which worsen renal function in residual kidney function and are contraindicated. Clinical pearl: The TWO conditions where corticosteroids are preferred for pericarditis per Ghana STG are: (1) Connective tissue disease-related pericarditis and (2) Uraemic pericarditis. For all other causes, NSAIDs plus colchicine is the first-line approach.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old man has a moderate pericardial effusion detected on echo. He is haemodynamically stable with no signs of tamponade. LV function is normal. What is the most appropriate immediate management?','["A. Emergency pericardiocentesis to drain the effusion", "B. Start anticoagulation with warfarin to prevent thrombus formation", "C. Treat the underlying cause, close monitoring with serial echocardiography, and NSAIDs plus colchicine if pericarditis is the cause", "D. Urgent surgical pericardiectomy"]','C','A haemodynamically stable patient with a moderate pericardial effusion and no tamponade physiology does not require emergency pericardiocentesis. Management focuses on identifying and treating the underlying cause (viral or idiopathic, TB, malignancy, uraemia) while monitoring with serial echocardiography. If pericarditis is the aetiology, NSAIDs plus colchicine are commenced. Pericardiocentesis (option A) is indicated for tamponade with haemodynamic compromise or for diagnostic sampling in large effusions of unclear aetiology. Anticoagulation (option C) is contraindicated in pericardial effusion as it risks haemopericardium and progression to tamponade. Pericardiectomy (option D) is for constrictive pericarditis, not pericardial effusion. Clinical pearl: Echo is the gold standard for assessing pericardial effusion. Look for right atrial and right ventricular diastolic collapse (early signs of tamponade) and inferior vena cava plethora defined as IVC not collapsing more than 50% with inspiration.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','hard','mcq','A 60-year-old man presents with hypotension (BP 80/60 mmHg), raised JVP, muffled heart sounds, and pulsus paradoxus of 18 mmHg. CXR shows an enlarged globular cardiac shadow. What is the emergency treatment?','["A. IV furosemide 80 mg and high-flow oxygen", "B. IV morphine and IV GTN to reduce preload and pain", "C. Start IV antibiotics and admit to ward for monitoring", "D. Echo-guided pericardiocentesis (needle aspiration of the pericardial effusion)"]','D','This patient has cardiac tamponade (Beck''s triad: hypotension, raised JVP, muffled heart sounds, plus pulsus paradoxus of 18 mmHg). Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency requiring immediate pericardiocentesis to relieve pericardial compression of the heart. Echo-guided pericardiocentesis via the subxiphoid approach is the standard technique. Furosemide (option A) would worsen hypotension by further reducing preload in a state where cardiac filling is already critically reduced. Morphine and GTN (option B) cause vasodilation and would be catastrophic in hypotensive tamponade. IV antibiotics (option D) may be needed for infective causes but do not address the immediate haemodynamic emergency. Clinical pearl: In tamponade, the right heart is compressed during diastole. The fixed pericardial volume means that as effusion accumulates, ventricular filling is impaired, reducing cardiac output progressively. A cautious IV fluid bolus can temporise while preparing for pericardiocentesis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old man with a history of tuberculosis presents with progressive dyspnoea, ascites, and bilateral lower limb oedema. JVP INCREASES with inspiration (Kussmaul''s sign). A pericardial knock is heard on auscultation. Pulsus paradoxus is absent. Echo shows a thickened pericardium. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Cardiac tamponade", "B. Constrictive pericarditis", "C. Dilated cardiomyopathy with biventricular failure", "D. Restrictive cardiomyopathy"]','B','Constrictive pericarditis is characterised by a thickened, scarred pericardium (from TB, radiation, or prior surgery) that restricts diastolic filling of all cardiac chambers. Key features: Kussmaul''s sign (paradoxical RISE in JVP during inspiration due to inability to increase RV volume when it cannot expand outward), pericardial knock (early diastolic sound from abrupt halt in ventricular filling), and signs of systemic venous congestion (ascites, oedema) out of proportion to pulmonary symptoms. Pulsus paradoxus is typically ABSENT in constrictive pericarditis (unlike tamponade). TB is the leading cause in Africa. Cardiac tamponade (option A) has pulsus paradoxus and acute presentation. Dilated cardiomyopathy (option B) has reduced EF. Restrictive cardiomyopathy (option D) can mimic constrictive pericarditis and requires CT/MRI pericardial thickness measurement and endomyocardial biopsy to distinguish. Clinical pearl: Constrictive pericarditis versus restrictive cardiomyopathy: pericardial thickness on CT greater than 4 mm favours constrictive, annular velocity on tissue Doppler greater than 8 cm/s favours constrictive.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','hard','mcq','A 40-year-old woman with SLE presents with pericarditis that has recurred three times despite adequate NSAID plus colchicine courses. Which treatment is most appropriate?','["A. Add prednisolone 0.2-0.5 mg/kg daily given the SLE aetiology", "B. Add aspirin 600 mg every 8 hours as sole monotherapy", "C. Double the colchicine dose to 1 mg four times daily for effect", "D. Refer for urgent pericardiectomy as all medical treatment has failed"]','A','Recurrent pericarditis in the context of SLE (a connective tissue disease) is one of the specific indications for corticosteroids per Ghana STG. Prednisolone at low-to-moderate doses (0.2-0.5 mg/kg/day) is appropriate because: (1) SLE is a CTD-related aetiology where steroids are indicated, (2) NSAIDs plus colchicine have failed after multiple courses, and (3) SLE activity may require immunosuppression for extracardiac manifestations. Aspirin monotherapy (option A) is insufficient for recurrent pericarditis in SLE. Doubling colchicine (option B) is not recommended and increases gastrointestinal toxicity without proven additional benefit. Pericardiectomy (option D) is a last resort for constrictive pericarditis or truly refractory recurrent pericarditis after all medical options are exhausted. Clinical pearl: When adding steroids for recurrent pericarditis always taper slowly. Rapid withdrawal can trigger a rebound attack. Taper over weeks to months guided by symptoms and CRP levels.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','hard','mcq','A 35-year-old man with TB pericarditis is started on anti-tuberculous therapy (RHZE). What adjunctive therapy should be added to prevent progression to constrictive pericarditis?','["A. Prednisolone 60 mg daily tapered over 11 weeks", "B. Aspirin 600 mg every 8 hours for 2-4 weeks", "C. Colchicine 0.5 mg twice daily for 3 months", "D. Warfarin to target INR 2-3"]','A','TB pericarditis has a high risk of progressing to constrictive pericarditis (up to 30-40%) without adjunctive corticosteroid therapy. Prednisolone at 60 mg per day initially, tapered over 11 weeks, is recommended as adjunctive therapy in TB pericarditis to reduce pericardial inflammation and prevent fibrosis and constriction. This is one of the specific indications for steroids in pericarditis per Ghana STG. Aspirin (option A) and colchicine (option B) are used for viral or idiopathic pericarditis but are not the standard adjunct for TB pericarditis. Warfarin (option D) has no role in preventing TB pericarditis complications. Clinical pearl: TB pericarditis management requires RHZE for 6 months plus a prednisolone taper. Despite this, up to 10-20% of patients still develop constrictive pericarditis and require pericardiectomy. Early diagnosis and treatment is critical to prevent this outcome.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','easy','mcq','An ankle-brachial pressure index (ABPI) of 0.65 measured in a patient with calf pain on walking most likely indicates:','["A. Normal peripheral circulation - ABPI 0.65 is within normal range", "B. Critical limb ischaemia requiring urgent intervention", "C. Mild peripheral arterial disease with claudication", "D. Venous insufficiency rather than arterial disease"]','C','The ankle-brachial pressure index (ABPI) interpretation: greater than 0.9 is normal, 0.7-0.9 is mild PAD and may be asymptomatic or with mild claudication, 0.4-0.7 is moderate PAD with claudication, and less than 0.4 is critical limb ischaemia with rest pain or tissue loss. An ABPI of 0.65 falls in the moderate PAD range and would typically cause claudication symptoms. Normal ABPI (option A) is greater than 0.9. Critical limb ischaemia (option C) requires ABPI less than 0.4 with rest pain or tissue loss. Venous insufficiency (option D) is not assessed by ABPI. It causes elevated venous pressures, oedema, and skin changes but does not reduce ABPI. Clinical pearl: ABPI greater than 1.3 suggests arterial calcification in diabetes or CKD, making the vessel non-compressible and the test unreliable. Use the toe-brachial index instead. ABPI is the most important non-invasive test for diagnosing PAD.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','easy','mcq','A 60-year-old man with diabetes and smoking history describes leg pain appearing after walking 200 metres that is relieved by rest within 5 minutes. This is classified as Fontaine stage:','["A. Stage I - asymptomatic PAD", "B. Stage II - intermittent claudication", "C. Stage III - ischaemic rest pain", "D. Stage IV - tissue loss with ulceration or gangrene"]','B','The Fontaine classification of peripheral arterial disease: Stage I is asymptomatic with reduced ABPI but no symptoms. Stage II is intermittent claudication with pain on exercise relieved by rest - Stage IIa if claudication distance is more than 200 m, Stage IIb if less than 200 m. Stage III is ischaemic rest pain especially at night relieved by hanging the leg down. Stage IV is tissue loss with non-healing ulcers or gangrene. This patient has pain on walking relieved by rest, which is Fontaine Stage II intermittent claudication. Stage I (option A) is asymptomatic. Stage III (option C) involves rest pain. Stage IV (option D) requires tissue necrosis. Clinical pearl: Claudication pain is reproducible at the same walking distance and resolves within 1-5 minutes of rest. Unlike spinal stenosis where relief requires sitting rather than just stopping, arterial claudication is relieved by simply standing still.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','easy','mcq','Which of the following is the strongest modifiable risk factor for peripheral arterial disease (PAD)?','["A. Hypertension", "B. Cigarette smoking", "C. Obesity", "D. Hypercholesterolaemia"]','B','Cigarette smoking is the most powerful modifiable risk factor for PAD. Smokers have a 2-4 fold higher risk than non-smokers and smoking accelerates disease progression. The relative risk of PAD attributable to smoking exceeds that from diabetes, hypertension, and hyperlipidaemia individually. Hypertension (option A) and hypercholesterolaemia (option D) are major risk factors but with lower attributable risk than smoking. Obesity (option B) acts mainly through its effects on other risk factors. Diabetes mellitus also confers very high PAD risk and is associated with distal below-knee disease and peripheral neuropathy masking symptoms. Clinical pearl: Smoking cessation is the most effective intervention to slow PAD progression and reduce cardiovascular events. It halves the risk of disease progression and the need for amputation. Every patient with PAD must be counselled on smoking cessation at every encounter.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old man with intermittent claudication has an ABPI of 0.6. He has stopped smoking and is on aspirin 75 mg daily and atorvastatin 40 mg daily. His claudication distance is now 100 metres. What is the next step?','["A. Recommend bed rest and elevation of both legs to improve circulation", "B. Urgent surgical bypass grafting of the affected limb", "C. Start warfarin anticoagulation to prevent thrombus progression", "D. Supervised exercise rehabilitation programme and consider cilostazol 100 mg twice daily"]','D','For stable intermittent claudication, the first-line intervention is a supervised exercise rehabilitation programme (walking to near-pain threshold for 30-60 minutes, 3 times per week for at least 3 months). This improves claudication distance by 100-200%. Cilostazol (a phosphodiesterase-3 inhibitor with antiplatelet and vasodilatory effects) at 100 mg twice daily can be added as adjunct pharmacotherapy to improve claudication distance. Bed rest and leg elevation (option A) are contraindicated in PAD as gravity helps perfusion in ischaemic limbs. Patients instinctively hang their legs down to improve perfusion. Surgical bypass (option C) is reserved for critical limb ischaemia or claudication significantly impairing quality of life despite medical therapy. Warfarin (option D) is not indicated for stable claudication. Clinical pearl: Cilostazol is contraindicated in heart failure of any severity as it inhibits PDE-3 and can worsen cardiac function. Always check for heart failure before prescribing.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old diabetic woman presents with a painful black necrotic area on her right big toe with surrounding cellulitis. Her ABPI is 0.35 and pedal pulses are absent. What is the most appropriate management?','["A. Optimise glycaemic control and apply local wound dressings only while awaiting review", "B. Oral antibiotics only and chiropody review in 4 weeks as an outpatient", "C. Start IV antibiotics, urgent vascular surgery referral for arterial revascularisation, and MDT wound care", "D. Primary amputation of the big toe without prior vascular assessment"]','C','This patient has critical limb ischaemia (CLI) defined by ABPI less than 0.4, with tissue loss (necrotic toe with cellulitis). CLI is a vascular emergency requiring: (1) IV antibiotics for the infected wound, (2) URGENT vascular surgery or interventional radiology referral for revascularisation (angioplasty or surgical bypass) to restore limb perfusion, and (3) Multidisciplinary wound care involving vascular surgeon, diabetologist, microbiologist, and podiatrist. Without revascularisation, the 1-year limb loss rate exceeds 30% and mortality is 20%. Local wound care alone (option A) is inadequate without addressing the underlying ischaemia. Oral antibiotics (option C) are insufficient for CLI with deep infection requiring IV therapy. Immediate amputation (option D) without vascular assessment is inappropriate as limb salvage should always be attempted first. Clinical pearl: CLI is defined as ABPI less than 0.4, rest pain for more than 2 weeks, or tissue loss (ulcer or gangrene). The Rutherford classification grades CLI severity in stages 4 through 6.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old man presents with sudden onset of severe left leg pain, pallor, coldness, and absent pulses below the femoral level. He has no previous history of claudication. ECG shows atrial fibrillation. What is the diagnosis and immediate management?','["A. Deep vein thrombosis - start anticoagulation and elevate the leg", "B. Vasospasm - apply warmth and give IV calcium gluconate", "C. Acute limb ischaemia from cardioembolism - IV heparin immediately and urgent vascular surgery referral", "D. Acute exacerbation of chronic PAD - supervised exercise and medical therapy"]','C','Acute limb ischaemia (ALI) presents with the 6 Ps: Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness, Paraesthesia, Paralysis, and Perishing cold (Poikilothermia). In a patient with AF, the most common aetiology is cardiac embolism from the left atrium or left atrial appendage. Immediate management: (1) IV unfractionated heparin bolus (5000 units) to prevent clot propagation and protect the microcirculation, and (2) Urgent vascular surgery referral for surgical embolectomy (Fogarty catheter technique) or catheter-directed thrombolysis within 6 hours. DVT (option A) causes venous signs with a swollen, blue, and warm limb rather than a pale, cold, and pulseless limb. Vasospasm (option C) causes Raynaud phenomenon and not sudden absent pulses in an AF patient. Chronic PAD (option D) does not present with sudden onset absent pulses. Clinical pearl: ALI is a vascular emergency as ischaemic muscle dies in 6 hours. Paralysis and paraesthesia are the most sinister features indicating motor and sensory nerve ischaemia requiring immediate revascularisation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','hard','mcq','A 72-year-old woman has critical limb ischaemia with a non-healing heel ulcer and ABPI 0.28. CT angiography shows a 10 cm occlusion of the superficial femoral artery with good distal run-off. Which revascularisation strategy is preferred as the first approach?','["A. Amputation below the knee as primary treatment given the severity", "B. Femoral-popliteal bypass surgery using a synthetic PTFE graft", "C. Conservative management with prostanoids (alprostadil) only", "D. Percutaneous transluminal angioplasty (PTA) with or without stenting of the SFA"]','D','For superficial femoral artery (SFA) disease with good distal run-off, endovascular therapy (PTA with or without stenting) is preferred as the first revascularisation approach due to lower procedural risk compared to open surgery, good early patency rates, and preservation of surgical options for future interventions. TASC II classification guides the choice: short lesions (TASC A and B) are treated endovascularly, while long complex lesions (TASC C and D, generally greater than 15 cm) may favour surgery. This 10 cm SFA occlusion is a TASC B to C lesion where endovascular therapy is a reasonable first approach. Primary amputation (option A) is only considered when revascularisation is not technically possible or the limb is non-viable. Femoral-popliteal bypass with synthetic graft (option C) is reserved for longer more complex disease or failed endovascular procedures. Note that autologous saphenous vein is preferred over synthetic grafts. Prostanoids alone (option D) are insufficient for CLI without revascularisation. Clinical pearl: Autologous vein bypass has better long-term patency than synthetic grafts in below-knee revascularisation. Always preserve the great saphenous vein.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','hard','mcq','A 68-year-old man with PAD undergoes a right femoral-popliteal bypass. Four days post-operatively he develops sudden severe pain in the right calf with a pale, cold leg. The graft is not palpable. What is the most likely diagnosis and management?','["A. Post-operative DVT - start therapeutic enoxaparin and elevate the leg", "B. Bypass graft occlusion - urgent return to theatre for surgical thrombectomy or revision", "C. Wound haematoma - apply pressure and observe for 24 hours", "D. Post-operative lymphoedema - compression bandaging and physiotherapy"]','B','Post-operative bypass graft occlusion presents with recurrence of ischaemic symptoms - sudden onset severe pain, pallor, and coldness of the bypassed limb with loss of the graft pulse. This is a vascular emergency requiring urgent return to theatre for surgical thrombectomy, graft revision or replacement, and investigation of the underlying cause such as technical problems, graft kinking, or poor outflow. Post-operative DVT (option A) causes a swollen, blue, venous limb rather than pale, cold arterial ischaemia, and enoxaparin would not restore graft patency. Wound haematoma (option C) causes local pain and swelling at the surgical site, not systemic limb ischaemia. Lymphoedema (option D) causes painless limb swelling without ischaemia. Clinical pearl: Early graft occlusion within 30 days is usually due to technical problems such as kinking, size mismatch, or poor anastomosis. Late occlusion after 30 days is due to neointimal hyperplasia or progression of atherosclerotic disease in the outflow vessels.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','hard','mcq','A 60-year-old woman with bilateral calf claudication has an ABPI of 0.85 on the right and 0.72 on the left. However, her symptoms are much more severe than the ABPI suggests, with inability to walk more than 50 metres. Which additional vascular test best explains the discrepancy?','["A. Post-exercise ABPI (ankle pressure measured immediately after standardised treadmill exercise)", "B. Duplex ultrasound of the abdominal aorta", "C. CT angiography of the chest and thoracic aorta", "D. MRI of the lumbar spine to exclude neurogenic claudication"]','A','In patients with claudication whose resting ABPI appears near-normal (0.72-0.85) but symptoms seem disproportionately severe, post-exercise ABPI is the most useful next test. Normal ABPI at rest does not exclude significant PAD as compensatory collateral vessels maintain resting pressure. Exercise unmasks haemodynamically significant stenoses. After a standardised treadmill test, a drop of greater than 20% in ankle pressure or greater than 20 mmHg confirms functionally significant PAD. Duplex ultrasound of the aorta (option A) is useful but does not explain the clinical-ABPI discrepancy. CT angiography of the chest (option C) maps thoracic anatomy and has no role here. MRI of the lumbar spine (option D) should be considered if neurogenic claudication is suspected as an important differential. Note that spinal claudication is relieved by SITTING rather than just stopping walking, as lumbar flexion widens the spinal canal. Clinical pearl: Neurogenic claudication from lumbar spinal stenosis has symptoms relieved by sitting or forward flexion, normal ABPI, and may present with bilateral leg weakness or paraesthesia in a non-vascular distribution.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old man presents with Buerger disease (thromboangiitis obliterans) affecting three fingers on his right hand. He continues to smoke. What is the cornerstone of management?','["A. Absolute smoking cessation - this is the only effective intervention to halt disease progression", "B. Start aspirin 75 mg and clopidogrel 75 mg dual antiplatelet therapy as the primary treatment", "C. Urgent coronary angiography to assess cardiac involvement of Buerger disease", "D. Start prednisolone 40 mg daily as Buerger disease is primarily an inflammatory vasculitis"]','A','Buerger disease (thromboangiitis obliterans) is a non-atherosclerotic, segmental inflammatory disease of small and medium arteries exclusively linked to tobacco use. The ONLY effective treatment is COMPLETE and ABSOLUTE smoking cessation - even a few cigarettes daily can perpetuate disease activity and lead to inexorable progression. Without cessation, amputation is inevitable. Revascularisation options are extremely limited due to the small vessel distal disease and poor distal run-off. Pharmacological therapy including antiplatelet agents, anticoagulation, and prostanoids has minimal evidence without smoking cessation. Dual antiplatelet therapy (option A) is used in atherosclerotic PAD but has limited benefit in Buerger disease without cessation. Coronary angiography (option C) is irrelevant as Buerger disease is a distal small vessel disease that does not affect coronary arteries. Steroids (option D) have no proven benefit and this is not a typical vasculitis responding to immunosuppression. Clinical pearl: Buerger disease typically affects young men under 45 years who are heavy smokers. It affects hands AND feet unlike atherosclerotic PAD which predominantly affects the lower limbs. Diagnosis requires exclusion of other causes of vasculopathy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','easy','mcq','A 45-year-old woman presents with unilateral right leg swelling, calf tenderness, and erythema after a long-haul flight. Her Wells DVT score is 3. What is the most appropriate next step?','["A. D-dimer assay alone is sufficient to diagnose DVT at this probability", "B. MRI venography of both legs", "C. X-ray of the right leg to exclude fracture", "D. Duplex compression ultrasound of the right leg"]','D','A Wells DVT score of ≥2 indicates high pre-test probability (greater than 56% risk of DVT), so imaging should proceed directly without waiting for D-dimer results. Compression duplex ultrasound is the first-line imaging modality for suspected DVT and is highly sensitive (greater than 95%) for proximal DVT. D-dimer alone (option A) is useful for RULING OUT DVT in low probability patients due to its high sensitivity and low specificity. It should not be used in isolation for high probability patients. MRI venography (option C) is rarely required and reserved for cases where ultrasound is inconclusive such as isolated iliac vein DVT. X-ray (option D) assesses bones but cannot diagnose DVT. Clinical pearl: In high probability DVT, start anticoagulation empirically while awaiting ultrasound if there will be a significant delay. Do not wait for imaging confirmation before treating when pre-test probability is high.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','easy','mcq','Which clinical scoring system is used to assess pre-test probability of pulmonary embolism?','["A. CURB-65 score", "B. Wells PE score", "C. CHADS2 score", "D. GRACE score"]','B','The Wells PE score is the standard tool for assessing pre-test probability of pulmonary embolism. It assigns points for: clinical signs and symptoms of DVT (3 points), PE being the most likely diagnosis (3 points), heart rate greater than 100 bpm (1.5 points), immobilisation or surgery in past 4 weeks (1.5 points), previous DVT or PE (1.5 points), haemoptysis (1 point), and malignancy (1 point). Score below 2 is low probability, 2-6 is intermediate, and above 6 is high probability. CURB-65 (option A) is used for pneumonia severity assessment. CHADS2 (option C) assesses stroke risk in AF. GRACE score (option D) is used for ACS risk stratification. Clinical pearl: The Wells criteria are combined with D-dimer to safely rule out PE in low-risk patients without CT pulmonary angiography. Wells score below 4 plus negative D-dimer effectively excludes PE.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','easy','mcq','What is the recommended duration of anticoagulation for a first unprovoked proximal DVT according to Ghana STG?','["A. 3-6 months with consideration of extended therapy given high recurrence risk", "B. 6 weeks only", "C. 3 months for all DVT regardless of provocation", "D. Lifelong anticoagulation is mandatory in all cases of DVT"]','A','Ghana STG recommends anticoagulation with warfarin (target INR 2-3) for 3-6 months for DVT treatment, with consideration of extended or indefinite therapy for unprovoked DVT due to the high recurrence risk (approximately 30% at 5 years without ongoing anticoagulation). Provoked DVT (after surgery, trauma, or immobilisation) is treated for 3 months as the provoking factor is removed. Unprovoked DVT warrants reassessment at 3 months, thrombophilia testing (antiphospholipid syndrome, factor V Leiden, protein C or S deficiency), and consideration of extended therapy. Six weeks (option A) is insufficient for any DVT. Three months alone (option B) is appropriate for provoked but not necessarily for unprovoked DVT. Lifelong anticoagulation for ALL cases (option D) is excessive. Clinical pearl: Before starting extended anticoagulation, consider bleeding risk using the HAS-BLED score. The benefit of preventing recurrent VTE must outweigh the annual bleeding risk of approximately 1-2% per year on warfarin.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old man presents with acute dyspnoea, pleuritic chest pain, and haemoptysis. BP is 110/70 mmHg, HR 108 bpm, SpO2 89% on air. Wells PE score is 7. CTPA confirms bilateral pulmonary emboli with no RV dilation. What is the management?','["A. Thrombolysis with IV alteplase immediately as the Wells score is high", "B. IV antibiotics for pneumonia as the most likely diagnosis in Ghana", "C. Immediate surgical pulmonary embolectomy", "D. Oxygen, analgesia (morphine), enoxaparin SC (or IV UFH), and warfarin as per Ghana STG for submassive PE"]','D','This patient has submassive (intermediate-risk) PE: haemodynamically stable (BP 110/70 mmHg, no shock or cardiac arrest) but symptomatic with hypoxaemia. Per Ghana STG, PE management includes: Oxygen to correct hypoxia, Morphine for analgesia and anxiolysis, Enoxaparin SC (1.5 mg/kg once daily or 1 mg/kg twice daily) or IV UFH, and Warfarin started simultaneously targeting INR 2-3, overlapping with heparin for a minimum of 5 days until INR is greater than 2 for 24 hours. Thrombolysis (option A) is reserved for MASSIVE PE with haemodynamic compromise (BP below 90 mmHg or cardiac arrest), NOT for stable submassive PE. Antibiotics (option C) treat pneumonia not PE. Surgical embolectomy (option D) is a last resort for massive PE failing thrombolysis. Clinical pearl: Hypotension with SBP below 90 mmHg or cardiac arrest equals MASSIVE PE requiring thrombolysis with alteplase. Haemodynamically stable equals SUBMASSIVE PE requiring anticoagulation only.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old woman with DVT is started on heparin and warfarin. After 5 days of heparin, her platelet count drops from 280,000 to 62,000 per microlitre. She develops extension of her DVT. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)", "B. Warfarin-induced skin necrosis from protein C depletion", "C. Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia type II (HIT)", "D. Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)"]','C','Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia type II (HIT) is an immune-mediated complication occurring 5-14 days after heparin exposure, characterised paradoxically by THROMBOSIS (both arterial and venous) despite a low platelet count. The 4T score (Thrombocytopenia, Timing, Thrombosis, oTher causes excluded) guides diagnosis. Key features: platelet drop greater than 50% (from 280k to 62k), timing on day 5, and new thrombosis (DVT extension) represent a high 4T score. Management: STOP ALL HEPARIN including LMWH and heparin flushes, and switch to a non-heparin anticoagulant (argatroban, fondaparinux, or danaparoid). DIC (option A) causes widespread bleeding not thrombosis. Warfarin skin necrosis (option C) occurs in protein C deficiency. TTP (option D) causes a pentad of thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia, neurological symptoms, renal failure, and fever. Clinical pearl: HIT is a PRO-THROMBOTIC state despite the low platelet count. NEVER transfuse platelets (worsens thrombosis) and NEVER restart heparin in any form in a patient with confirmed HIT.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old man on warfarin (INR 2.5) for AF develops a new DVT. What does this indicate?','["A. The warfarin dose should be halved as it is causing paradoxical thrombosis", "B. This is expected on warfarin - no action needed beyond continuing current therapy", "C. DVT on therapeutic anticoagulation suggests underlying malignancy or thrombophilia requiring investigation", "D. Switch to aspirin 300 mg daily as warfarin has clearly failed"]','C','DVT occurring despite therapeutic anticoagulation (INR 2-3 on warfarin) is a red flag for an underlying prothrombotic state, most commonly occult malignancy (approximately 10-20% of idiopathic VTE is associated with cancer) or a thrombophilia (antiphospholipid syndrome, protein C or S deficiency, antithrombin deficiency, factor V Leiden, or JAK2 mutation). Investigation should include: malignancy screening (history, clinical examination, CT chest, abdomen, and pelvis), thrombophilia screen (ideally before anticoagulation or 3 months after stopping), and antiphospholipid antibodies. Halving warfarin (option A) is contraindicated and worsens thrombosis. Simply observing (option B) ignores a potentially life-threatening underlying pathology. Aspirin (option D) is inadequate for active DVT. Clinical pearl: Trousseau syndrome is recurrent migratory thrombophlebitis classically associated with pancreatic and other visceral malignancies. VTE on therapeutic anticoagulation should always prompt a systematic cancer search.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','hard','mcq','A 58-year-old woman presents with massive PE: BP 70/40 mmHg, HR 130 bpm, SpO2 78% on high-flow oxygen, and RV dilation with D-shaped septum on bedside echo. She has no contraindications to thrombolysis. What is the most appropriate immediate treatment?','["A. IV unfractionated heparin 5000 units bolus and commence warfarin alone", "B. Surgical pulmonary embolectomy immediately without thrombolysis", "C. Enoxaparin SC 1 mg/kg and oral rivaroxaban as initial treatment", "D. IV alteplase 10 mg bolus then 90 mg over 2 hours with IV UFH commenced post-thrombolysis"]','D','Massive PE is defined by haemodynamic compromise (SBP below 90 mmHg for more than 15 minutes) with confirmed PE, and carries greater than 15% in-hospital mortality. Systemic thrombolysis with IV alteplase (100 mg total: 10 mg IV bolus then 90 mg over 2 hours) is the treatment of choice for massive PE without absolute contraindications. IV UFH is started immediately after thrombolysis without a loading bolus. UFH alone (option A) is insufficient for haemodynamically compromised massive PE requiring immediate thrombus dissolution. Surgical embolectomy (option B) is reserved for failure of or absolute contraindications to thrombolysis. LMWH plus DOAC (option D) are not appropriate for massive PE requiring immediate haemodynamic reversal. Clinical pearl: Contraindications to thrombolysis in PE are similar to those in STEMI: prior haemorrhagic stroke, active bleeding, recent surgery. In cardiac arrest from PE, thrombolysis is given empirically even without confirmed diagnosis. Continue CPR for 60-90 minutes to allow drug effect.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','hard','mcq','A 75-year-old man develops a DVT three months after colon cancer resection. He is on LMWH. An IVC filter is being considered. Which is an absolute indication for an IVC filter?','["A. Recurrent DVT on therapeutic anticoagulation", "B. PE in a patient with an absolute contraindication to anticoagulation such as active life-threatening haemorrhage", "C. Large iliofemoral DVT with floating thrombus", "D. Known thrombophilia with a first DVT on anticoagulation"]','B','The only ABSOLUTE indication for an IVC filter is PE (or high-risk proximal DVT) in a patient who has an absolute contraindication to anticoagulation, such as active life-threatening haemorrhage, recent neurosurgery, or severe thrombocytopenia precluding anticoagulation. IVC filters prevent further pulmonary emboli but do not treat the existing clot and do not replace anticoagulation when it can be safely used. Recurrent DVT on anticoagulation (option A) is an indication to intensify anticoagulation or switch to LMWH (especially in malignancy), not necessarily an IVC filter. Large iliofemoral DVT (option B) alone is not an indication. Thrombophilia with DVT (option D) requires anticoagulation optimisation, not a filter. Clinical pearl: Retrievable IVC filters should be removed as soon as the contraindication to anticoagulation resolves, typically within 30-180 days. Permanent filters increase long-term DVT risk due to filter thrombosis and venous stasis below the filter level.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','hard','mcq','A pregnant woman at 28 weeks gestation presents with confirmed proximal DVT. Which anticoagulant is most appropriate?','["A. Low-molecular-weight heparin (enoxaparin 1 mg/kg SC twice daily) throughout pregnancy and for 6 weeks postpartum", "B. Warfarin - start immediately and monitor INR weekly throughout pregnancy", "C. Dabigatran 150 mg twice daily for 6 months and then reassess", "D. Aspirin 300 mg daily for 3 months"]','A','Low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) is the anticoagulant of choice for VTE in pregnancy. It does not cross the placenta due to its large molecular size, has predictable pharmacokinetics in pregnancy, and does not cause warfarin embryopathy. The dose is enoxaparin 1 mg/kg SC twice daily (therapeutic dosing), continued throughout pregnancy and for at least 6 weeks postpartum, with a total minimum of 3 months treatment. Warfarin (option A) crosses the placenta and is teratogenic in the first trimester (warfarin embryopathy: nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses, IUGR) and causes fetal intracranial haemorrhage near delivery. Direct oral anticoagulants including dabigatran (option B) and rivaroxaban are contraindicated in pregnancy due to placental transfer and lack of safety data. Aspirin (option D) is not therapeutic for active DVT. Clinical pearl: Warfarin is safe after delivery for breastfeeding mothers as it does not transfer significantly to breast milk. LMWH or warfarin can both be used postpartum.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','hard','mcq','A 50-year-old man is on therapeutic heparin for DVT. On day 8, his platelet count has fallen from 320,000 to 48,000 per microlitre and a serotonin release assay is positive. He develops new arterial thrombosis in the right arm. What is the correct management?','["A. Stop all heparin immediately, start a non-heparin anticoagulant (argatroban or fondaparinux), and do NOT transfuse platelets", "B. Continue heparin at the same dose and transfuse platelets to maintain count above 50,000", "C. Reduce heparin dose by 50% and recheck platelet count in 24 hours", "D. Stop heparin and start warfarin immediately to maintain anticoagulation"]','A','This patient has confirmed HIT type II (positive serotonin release assay, platelet drop greater than 50% on day 8 of heparin, and new arterial thrombosis). Management requires: (1) STOP ALL HEPARIN immediately including LMWH, heparin flushes, and heparin-coated catheters, (2) Start a non-heparin anticoagulant such as argatroban (a direct thrombin inhibitor) in non-renally impaired patients, or fondaparinux as an alternative, and (3) Do NOT transfuse platelets as this risks propagating the platelet-antibody-driven thrombus. Continuing heparin (option A) perpetuates the immune reaction. Reducing heparin dose (option C) does not stop the immune-mediated platelet activation. Starting warfarin immediately (option D) is CONTRAINDICATED in active HIT. Warfarin depletes protein C before its anticoagulant effect begins, potentially worsening thrombosis and causing warfarin-induced limb gangrene. Clinical pearl: In HIT - STOP HEPARIN, NO PLATELETS, NO WARFARIN until platelet count recovers above 150,000, START argatroban or fondaparinux. After platelet recovery, slowly introduce warfarin with adequate overlap.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','easy','mcq','Shock is best defined as:','["A. Systolic blood pressure below 90 mmHg on two readings", "B. Heart rate greater than 100 beats per minute", "C. Inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygen delivery to meet cellular metabolic demands", "D. Loss of more than one litre of blood from any source"]','C','Shock is defined as a state of acute circulatory failure resulting in inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygen delivery to meet cellular metabolic demands, leading to cellular hypoxia and organ dysfunction. The definition is physiological, not based on any single numerical threshold. Blood pressure can be maintained in early shock by compensatory mechanisms. Low BP (option A) may occur in shock but is not the definition as some patients maintain BP even in severe shock. Tachycardia (option B) is a compensatory response to falling cardiac output, not the definition of shock. Blood loss (option D) may cause haemorrhagic shock but is a cause, not the definition. Clinical pearl: The four types of shock are: (1) Hypovolaemic (low preload), (2) Distributive (low SVR - septic, anaphylactic, neurogenic), (3) Cardiogenic (low CO with high SVR), and (4) Obstructive (mechanical obstruction - PE, tamponade, tension pneumothorax).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','easy','mcq','Which haemodynamic profile is characteristic of septic (distributive) shock?','["A. Low cardiac output, high SVR, high PCWP", "B. High cardiac output, low SVR, low PCWP", "C. Low cardiac output, high SVR, low PCWP", "D. High cardiac output, high SVR, high PCWP"]','B','Septic (distributive) shock is characterised by massive vasodilation: high cardiac output (hyperdynamic state from tachycardia and increased stroke volume initially), low systemic vascular resistance (SVR) from inflammatory mediator-induced vasodilation, and low pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) due to venodilation and relative hypovolaemia. Option A (low CO, high SVR, high PCWP) describes cardiogenic shock. Option C (low CO, high SVR, low PCWP) describes hypovolaemic shock. Option D (high CO, high SVR) does not correspond to a recognised shock pattern. Clinical pearl: Haemodynamic profiles by Swan-Ganz catheter: Hypovolaemic has low CO, high SVR, low PCWP. Cardiogenic has low CO, high SVR, and HIGH PCWP. Distributive has HIGH CO, low SVR, low PCWP. Obstructive has low CO, high SVR, and variable PCWP depending on the cause.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','easy','mcq','A 30-year-old man is brought to ED after a road traffic accident with suspected intra-abdominal bleeding. HR is 128 bpm and BP is 82/50 mmHg. His skin is cold and clammy. What type of shock is this?','["A. Distributive (septic) shock", "B. Hypovolaemic (haemorrhagic) shock", "C. Cardiogenic shock", "D. Obstructive shock"]','B','Hypovolaemic or haemorrhagic shock results from acute volume loss - blood loss in this trauma setting. It presents with tachycardia, hypotension, cold clammy skin (compensatory vasoconstriction maintaining skin perfusion at the cost of peripheral constriction), decreased urine output, and altered consciousness in severe cases. ATLS classification grades haemorrhage: Class I is less than 15% blood loss, Class II is 15-30%, Class III is 30-40%, and Class IV is more than 40%. Distributive shock (option A) typically presents with WARM flushed skin due to vasodilation. Cardiogenic shock (option B) has a cardiac cause with signs of pulmonary congestion. Obstructive shock (option C) has a mechanical cause such as tension pneumothorax or tamponade. Clinical pearl: Initial management of haemorrhagic shock uses balanced resuscitation with 1:1:1 packed red cells to FFP to platelets. Avoid massive crystalloid resuscitation which causes dilutional coagulopathy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old woman with known heart failure presents with worsening dyspnoea and hypotension (BP 78/50 mmHg). She has cold extremities, elevated JVP, bilateral basal crackles, and HR 118 bpm. Bedside echo shows severely reduced LV function (EF 18%). Which vasopressor or inotrope combination is most appropriate?','["A. IV noradrenaline 0.1-0.3 mcg/kg/min as the sole agent", "B. IV adrenaline 0.5 mg subcutaneously every 5 minutes", "C. IV dopamine at high dose 15-20 mcg/kg/min", "D. IV dobutamine 5-10 mcg/kg/min with a low-dose vasopressor added if MAP remains below 65 mmHg"]','D','Cardiogenic shock with severely reduced EF (18%) requires inotropic support with dobutamine as first-line per Ghana STG. Dobutamine (beta-1 inotrope plus beta-2 vasodilator) increases CO while reducing afterload. If MAP remains below 65 mmHg despite dobutamine, a low-dose vasopressor (noradrenaline or dopamine at low-intermediate doses) can be cautiously added. Noradrenaline alone (option A) increases afterload through alpha-1 agonism, worsening cardiac function in pure cardiogenic shock. It should not be used as the sole first-line agent. Adrenaline SC (option C) is for anaphylaxis, not cardiogenic shock. High-dose dopamine (option D) at more than 10 mcg/kg/min causes pure vasoconstriction (alpha-1 dominant), worsening afterload and cardiac function. Clinical pearl: Dopamine pharmacology is dose-dependent: 1-3 mcg/kg/min is dopaminergic (renal and splanchnic vasodilation), 3-10 mcg/kg/min is beta-1 inotropic, and greater than 10 mcg/kg/min is alpha-1 vasoconstrictive.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','medium','mcq','A 40-year-old man develops sudden cardiovascular collapse following a bee sting. BP is 60/30 mmHg, he has facial angioedema, urticaria, and bronchospasm. What is the MOST IMPORTANT immediate treatment?','["A. IV chlorphenamine 10 mg as the first-line antihistamine", "B. IV hydrocortisone 200 mg immediately", "C. IM adrenaline (epinephrine) 0.5 mg (1:1000) into the outer thigh", "D. IV salbutamol 250 mcg for the bronchospasm"]','C','Anaphylactic shock requires immediate IM adrenaline (epinephrine) 0.5 mg (1:1000 solution equals 0.5 mL) into the anterolateral thigh as the first and most critical intervention. Adrenaline reverses all features of anaphylaxis through alpha-1 effects (vasoconstriction, reverses hypotension), beta-1 effects (increased heart rate and contractility), and beta-2 effects (bronchodilation, reduces urticaria and angioedema). IV antihistamine (chlorphenamine, option A) is adjunctive and does NOT save lives in anaphylaxis on its own. Hydrocortisone (option B) takes 4-6 hours to work and is adjunctive to prevent biphasic reactions. IV salbutamol (option D) addresses bronchospasm specifically but not the cardiovascular collapse. Clinical pearl: IM adrenaline in anaphylaxis is given into the anterolateral THIGH (vastus lateralis) for faster absorption than the deltoid muscle. IV adrenaline (1:10,000) is only used in cardiac arrest or by experienced practitioners with continuous monitoring.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','medium','mcq','A 28-year-old man is brought in after a high-speed road traffic accident. He develops sudden respiratory distress with absent breath sounds on the right, tracheal deviation to the LEFT, and BP drops to 70 mmHg. What is the immediate management?','["A. Urgent CXR to confirm the diagnosis before any intervention", "B. IV morphine and close observation for 30 minutes to assess progress", "C. Immediate needle decompression of the RIGHT chest at the 2nd intercostal space, mid-clavicular line", "D. CTPA to exclude pulmonary embolism as the cause"]','C','Tension pneumothorax is a CLINICAL diagnosis requiring IMMEDIATE needle decompression WITHOUT waiting for imaging. The triad of absent breath sounds on the right, tracheal deviation AWAY from the tension (to the left), and haemodynamic collapse (BP 70 mmHg) confirms tension pneumothorax. Immediate treatment: large-bore IV cannula (14G) inserted at the 2nd intercostal space (ICS) at the midclavicular line on the affected side (right). A rush of air confirms the diagnosis. Follow with chest drain insertion. Waiting for CXR (option A) or CTPA (option D) risks cardiac arrest from obstructive shock. Morphine (option C) does not address the obstructive physiology. Clinical pearl: The 5th intercostal space at the anterior axillary line is an alternative decompression site with better success rate in muscular patients where the 2nd ICS needle may not reach the pleural space through chest wall thickness. Tension pneumothorax equals obstructive shock and requires immediate needle decompression.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','hard','mcq','A 70-year-old man with septic shock due to a urinary tract infection has been given 30 mL/kg IV crystalloid. His MAP remains 58 mmHg. Which vasopressor should be used as first-line?','["A. IV vasopressin 0.4 units/min as the preferred first-line agent", "B. IV adrenaline 0.05-0.3 mcg/kg/min as the most potent vasopressor", "C. IV phenylephrine 100-400 mcg/min as a pure alpha agonist", "D. IV noradrenaline 0.01-0.5 mcg/kg/min to target MAP ≥65 mmHg"]','D','Per Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines, noradrenaline is the FIRST-LINE vasopressor in septic shock. It primarily acts on alpha-1 receptors to increase SVR (correcting the pathological vasodilation of septic shock) and has some beta-1 activity to maintain cardiac output. The target MAP is ≥65 mmHg (individualised to 80 mmHg in known hypertensives). Vasopressin (option A) is a second-line agent added to noradrenaline when refractory septic shock requires additional vasopressor support. Adrenaline (option B) is a second-line vasopressor and inotrope with higher arrhythmic risk and lactic acidosis. Phenylephrine (option D) is a pure alpha-1 agonist that increases SVR but reduces CO by increasing afterload. It is used in vasodilatory shock when tachycardia is problematic. Clinical pearl: Noradrenaline is first-line in septic shock because it corrects the pathophysiology (vasodilation) without excessive tachycardia. The SOAP II trial showed higher arrhythmia rates with dopamine compared to noradrenaline, establishing noradrenaline as the standard.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','hard','mcq','A 65-year-old man with cardiogenic shock post-MI has MAP 55 mmHg despite dobutamine 10 mcg/kg/min. An intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) is placed. What is the mechanism of benefit of IABP in cardiogenic shock?','["A. It directly increases cardiac contractility by electrical stimulation of the myocardium", "B. Diastolic inflation increases coronary perfusion pressure and systolic deflation reduces afterload and myocardial oxygen demand", "C. It inflates during systole to physically augment cardiac output", "D. It replaces cardiac function entirely acting as a ventricular assist device"]','B','The intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) works by counterpulsation: the balloon DEFLATES during systole, reducing afterload (the pressure the LV must overcome to eject blood), decreasing myocardial oxygen demand, and improving stroke volume. The balloon INFLATES during diastole (when the aortic valve is closed), increasing diastolic aortic pressure and improving coronary artery perfusion, which occurs predominantly during diastole. This dual mechanism reduces cardiac workload while improving myocardial oxygen supply. The IABP does NOT directly stimulate contractility (option A), does not inflate during systole (option B, which would dangerously increase afterload), and does not replace cardiac function (option D, which describes an LVAD or ECMO). Clinical pearl: Despite the physiological rationale, the IABP-SHOCK II trial showed IABP did not reduce 30-day mortality in cardiogenic shock compared to medical therapy alone. Current ESC guidelines have downgraded its routine use but it remains in use as an adjunct to other therapies.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','hard','mcq','A 35-year-old woman develops shock after receiving IV contrast dye. BP is 65/40 mmHg, she is flushed and warm, HR 140 bpm. Despite IM adrenaline 0.5 mg and 1 litre of IV crystalloid, BP is only 75/45 mmHg. What is the next step?','["A. Repeat IM adrenaline 0.5 mg, give IV fluid bolus 500 mL rapidly, and consider IV adrenaline infusion if no response", "B. Give IV chlorphenamine 20 mg and observe for 30 minutes", "C. Start oral prednisolone 40 mg daily", "D. Administer IV salbutamol 250 mcg and nebulised ipratropium"]','A','In refractory anaphylactic shock after initial IM adrenaline, the next steps are: repeat the IM adrenaline dose (0.5 mg IM every 5-15 minutes up to 3 doses), continue aggressive IV fluid resuscitation (up to 1-2 litres of crystalloid for adults), and if hypotension persists, commence IV adrenaline infusion (0.05-0.5 mcg/kg/min) with continuous cardiac monitoring. Antihistamine alone (option A) has no role in haemodynamic resuscitation in anaphylaxis. Oral prednisolone (option C) takes 4-6 hours to act and is adjunctive to prevent biphasic reactions, not for acute resuscitation. IV salbutamol (option D) addresses bronchospasm but does not reverse cardiovascular collapse. Clinical pearl: Anaphylaxis resistant to IM adrenaline may be due to: inadequate or delayed dose, patient on beta-blockers reducing adrenaline efficacy. For beta-blocker-induced adrenaline resistance, IV glucagon 1-2 mg is the specific antidote as it bypasses beta-receptors and stimulates adenylyl cyclase directly.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old man with septic shock has been on noradrenaline 0.4 mcg/kg/min for 12 hours. Despite adequate fluid resuscitation and source control, MAP remains 60 mmHg. Random serum cortisol is 180 nmol/L. What should be added?','["A. IV hydrocortisone 200-300 mg per day (50 mg every 6 hours) for 5-7 days", "B. IV dexamethasone 10 mg stat and continue daily", "C. Increase noradrenaline to maximum dose and add adrenaline as second vasopressor", "D. Add vasopressin 0.04 units/min without reducing noradrenaline"]','A','Refractory septic shock (requiring high-dose vasopressors despite adequate resuscitation and source control) is an indication for IV hydrocortisone per Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines. The dose is hydrocortisone 200 mg per day (either 50 mg every 6 hours or 200 mg as a continuous infusion) for 5-7 days, then weaned. A random cortisol of 180 nmol/L is low, suggesting relative adrenal insufficiency (critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency, CIRCI). Corticosteroids reduce vasopressor requirement and allow faster resolution of shock. Dexamethasone (option A) lacks mineralocorticoid activity and interferes with the cortisol assay if further testing is needed. Maximal noradrenaline plus adrenaline (option C) increases mortality risk without treating the underlying adrenal insufficiency. Vasopressin (option D) is a reasonable second vasopressor but does not address the cortisol deficiency. Clinical pearl: Fludrocortisone (50 mcg per day orally) can be added to hydrocortisone in septic shock for mineralocorticoid effect per the APROCCHSS trial, though hydrocortisone alone has some mineralocorticoid activity at the doses used.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
